window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-plant-kingdom","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Plant Kingdom · Practice","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Plants of this group are diploid and well adapted to extreme conditions. They grow bearing sporophylls in compact structures called cones. The group in reference is","image":"","options":["Monocots","Dicots","Pteridophytes","Gymnosperms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Gymnosperms are diploid sporophytes with xerophytic adaptations, such as thick cuticles and sunken stomata. These help them survive extreme conditions.\nThey bear microsporophylls and megasporophylls in compact strobili, or cones.\nSome pteridophytes, such as Selaginella and Equisetum, also have strobili. However, they depend on water for fertilisation and are not well adapted to harsh conditions.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The male sex organ of a bryophyte and the gametes it produces are, respectively:","image":"","options":["Archegonium; non-motile eggs","Antheridium; biflagellate antherozoids","Antheridium; non-flagellated male gametes carried by a pollen tube","Sporangium; flagellated zoospores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: in bryophytes the male sex organ is the antheridium. It produces biflagellate antherozoids, which swim in water to reach the egg in the archegonium.\nThe archegonium is the female organ, so (1) is wrong.\nNon-flagellated gametes carried by a pollen tube occur in seed plants, so (3) is wrong.\nA sporangium is not a sex organ, and zoospores are asexual spores, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for structures in gymnosperms.\nList-I\n(a) Nucellus\n(b) Pollen grain\n(c) Megasporophyll\n(d) Microsporangium\nList-II\n(i) Bears ovules and may be clustered into female cones\n(ii) Site where pollen grains develop\n(iii) Reduced male gametophyte\n(iv) Tissue from one of whose cells the megaspore mother cell differentiates","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Nucellus → (iv): one cell of the nucellus differentiates into the megaspore mother cell.\n(b) Pollen grain → (iii): it is the highly reduced male gametophyte.\n(c) Megasporophyll → (i): it bears ovules, and megasporophylls may be clustered into female cones.\n(d) Microsporangium → (ii): microspores formed here develop into pollen grains.\nSo the matching is a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii. Option (2) fails because the nucellus does not produce pollen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, roughly what share of the total carbon dioxide fixation on earth is carried out by algae through photosynthesis?","image":"","options":["Less than one-tenth","About one-fourth","At least one-half","Nearly all of it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that algae carry out at least half of the total carbon dioxide fixation on earth through photosynthesis.\nThis makes them primary producers of global importance in aquatic habitats, not minor contributors.\nOptions 1 and 2 underestimate this share.\nOption 4 overstates it, because land plants also fix a large amount of carbon.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A pteridophyte bears small leaves (microphylls) and compact strobili, and it produces two kinds of spores, large and small. Which option correctly identifies the plant and its class?","image":"","options":["Equisetum – Sphenopsida","Salvinia – Pteropsida","Lycopodium – Lycopsida","Selaginella – Lycopsida"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: Selaginella (class Lycopsida) has small leaves (microphylls), bears compact strobili and is heterosporous. It produces large megaspores and small microspores.\nWhy the other options fail:\n- Equisetum (Sphenopsida) forms strobili but is homosporous.\n- Lycopodium is in Lycopsida too, but NCERT does not list it as heterosporous.\n- Salvinia is heterosporous, but it is a fern and does not fit the description of microphylls and compact strobili.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A microbiologist prepares a solid culture medium using a commercial product obtained from Gracilaria. Which feature is correctly associated with the class to which this alga belongs?","image":"","options":["Food is stored as laminarin or mannitol","Chlorophyll a and b dominate, and pyrenoids store starch","Food is stored as floridean starch, and flagellated cells are absent","Asexual reproduction is by pear-shaped biflagellate zoospores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The commercial product used to solidify culture media is agar, which is obtained from Gracilaria. Gracilaria belongs to Rhodophyceae (red algae).\nRed algae store food as floridean starch and have no flagellated cells. So (3) fits.\nOptions (1) and (4) describe brown algae: laminarin or mannitol as food, and pear-shaped biflagellate zoospores.\nOption (2) describes green algae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines a filamentous green alga during sexual reproduction. She finds that the fusing gametes are flagellated and similar in size. The alga and its mode of sexual reproduction are most likely","image":"","options":["Spirogyra, isogamy","Eudorina, anisogamy","Volvox, oogamy","Ulothrix, isogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Gametes of similar size mean isogamy.\nAmong filamentous algae, Ulothrix produces flagellated isogametes, which matches the observation.\nSpirogyra is also filamentous and isogamous, but its gametes are non-flagellated, so (1) fails.\nEudorina (anisogamy) and Volvox (oogamy) do not show similar-sized gametes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (genus) with List-II (class of pteridophytes) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\n(A) Psilotum\n(B) Lycopodium\n(C) Equisetum\n(D) Adiantum\nList-II\n(I) Sphenopsida\n(II) Pteropsida\n(III) Psilopsida\n(IV) Lycopsida","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT divides pteridophytes into four classes.\n(A) Psilotum belongs to Psilopsida, so A-III.\n(B) Lycopodium belongs to Lycopsida, along with Selaginella, so B-IV.\n(C) Equisetum (horsetail) belongs to Sphenopsida, so C-I.\n(D) Adiantum is a fern of Pteropsida, along with Pteris and Dryopteris, so D-II.\nOption (2) wrongly swaps Psilotum and Lycopodium. Option (4) wrongly swaps Lycopodium and Equisetum.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Gelidium\nB. Sargassum\nC. Chlorella\nD. Sphagnum\nList-II\n(i) Source of peat used as fuel and packing material\n(ii) Source of agar\n(iii) Protein-rich food supplement used by space travellers\n(iv) Marine brown alga used as food\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Gelidium → (ii): it is a red alga used as a source of agar.\nB. Sargassum → (iv): it is a marine brown alga eaten as food.\nC. Chlorella → (iii): it is a unicellular, protein-rich supplement used even by space travellers.\nD. Sphagnum → (i): this moss provides peat, used as fuel and packing material.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mosses, along with lichens, are among the first organisms to colonise bare rocks, and they decompose rock to make the substrate suitable for higher plants.\nStatement II: Mosses increase soil erosion because their dense mats prevent rainwater from soaking into the soil.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mosses and lichens are pioneers on bare rock. They decompose the rock and make the substrate suitable for higher plants.\nStatement II is incorrect. Dense moss mats reduce the impact of falling rain, which prevents soil erosion rather than increasing it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Both gymnosperms and angiosperms produce seeds. Which feature separates angiosperms from gymnosperms?","image":"","options":["Angiosperms are heterosporous, while gymnosperms are homosporous","In angiosperms the ovules are enclosed and the seeds are covered within fruits, while in gymnosperms the ovules and seeds are exposed","Angiosperms have a dominant sporophyte, while gymnosperms have a dominant gametophyte","Only angiosperms possess xylem and phloem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Gymnosperms ('naked seeds') have ovules not enclosed by an ovary wall, so their seeds remain exposed.\nIn angiosperms the ovules lie within the ovary, and the seeds are enclosed in fruits.\nThe other options fail: both groups are heterosporous, both have a dominant sporophyte, and both have xylem and phloem.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The embryo sac of an Angiosperm is made up of","image":"","options":["8 cells","7 cells and 8 nuclei","8 cells and 8 nuclei","7 cells and 7 nuclei"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A typical angiosperm embryo sac contains 3 antipodals, 2 synergids, 1 egg cell and 1 central cell.\nThat makes 7 cells in total.\nThe central cell holds 2 polar nuclei, so the 7 cells contain 8 nuclei.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about angiosperms is correct?","image":"","options":["They are divided into two classes, the dicotyledons and the monocotyledons","They are divided into four classes: Psilopsida, Lycopsida, Sphenopsida and Pteropsida","They are restricted to narrow geographical regions because they need water for fertilisation","Their pollen grains and ovules develop in strobili, and their seeds remain naked"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT divides angiosperms (flowering plants) into two classes: dicotyledons and monocotyledons.\nPsilopsida, Lycopsida, Sphenopsida and Pteropsida are the classes of pteridophytes.\nNeeding water for fertilisation, which limits spread, is true of pteridophytes, not angiosperms.\nStrobili with naked seeds describe gymnosperms. Angiosperm seeds are enclosed in fruits.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The main, conspicuous plant body of a bryophyte is:","image":"","options":["diploid and spore-producing (sporophyte)","haploid and gamete-producing (gametophyte)","diploid and gamete-producing (gametophyte)","haploid and spore-producing (sporophyte)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the main plant body of a bryophyte is haploid.\nIt bears sex organs and produces gametes, so it is called the gametophyte.\nOption (1) describes pteridophytes, where the dominant body is the diploid sporophyte. That is the usual confusion.\nOptions (3) and (4) mismatch ploidy and function: a gametophyte is haploid and a sporophyte is diploid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes a flat, dorsiventral, green thallus of a liverwort. Small cup-like receptacles on its upper surface contain tiny green bodies, and rhizoids are present on its lower surface. Which statement about these tiny green bodies is correct?","image":"","options":["They are diploid buds formed after fertilisation","They are haploid, multicellular asexual buds that detach and grow into new individuals","They are unicellular spores produced by meiosis in the capsule","They are male sex organs that release biflagellate antherozoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cup-like receptacles are gemma cups, and the green bodies inside them are gemmae.\nGemmae are green, multicellular asexual buds. They detach and grow into new plants.\nThey form on the thallus, which is the haploid gametophyte, so gemmae are haploid.\nOption (3) fails because meiotic spores form only in the capsule of the sporophyte.\nOption (1) fails because gemmae are not formed after fertilisation and are not diploid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following algae is an example of a colonial form?","image":"","options":["Spirogyra","Ulothrix","Volvox","Laminaria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes algae in three forms: colonial (Volvox), filamentous (Ulothrix, Spirogyra) and massive (kelps).\nVolvox is a colony, a spherical group of many cells living together, so it fits.\nSpirogyra and Ulothrix are the tempting options, but both are unbranched filaments, not colonies.\nLaminaria is a large brown alga (kelp) with a massive thallus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A student describes a marine alga with these features:\n- an olive-brown body attached to rocks by a holdfast, with a stalk and a leaf-like photosynthetic frond;\n- asexual reproduction by pear-shaped zoospores bearing two unequal, laterally attached flagella.\nIn what form does this alga most likely store its food?","image":"","options":["Floridean starch","Starch in pyrenoids","Laminarin or mannitol","Glycogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Three clues identify a brown alga (Phaeophyceae): the olive-brown colour, a body divided into holdfast, stipe and frond, and pear-shaped zoospores with two unequal, laterally attached flagella.\nBrown algae store food as complex carbohydrates, namely laminarin or mannitol.\nFloridean starch (option 1) is the reserve of red algae. Starch in pyrenoids (option 2) is typical of green algae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are correctly attributed to the liverwort Marchantia?\n(i) Thallus is dorsiventral\n(ii) Thallus is closely appressed to the substrate\n(iii) Gemma cups are present on the thallus\n(iv) Upright, slender axes bear spirally arranged leaves\n(v) A protonema stage develops directly from the spore\n(vi) Sporophyte consists of foot, seta and capsule","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the Marchantia thallus is dorsiventral.\n(ii) Correct: the thallus is closely appressed to the substrate.\n(iii) Correct: gemma cups are present on the thallus.\n(iv) Incorrect: upright axes with spirally arranged leaves describe the moss leafy stage.\n(v) Incorrect: a protonema stage is a moss feature. In liverworts the spore germinates directly into the gametophyte.\n(vi) Correct: the liverwort sporophyte has a foot, seta and capsule.\nFour features are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The colour of brown algae varies from olive green to various shades of brown. Which pigment determines this variation, depending on how much of it is present?","image":"","options":["r-Phycoerythrin","Fucoxanthin","Chlorophyll b","Chlorophyll d"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: brown algae (Phaeophyceae) contain chlorophyll a, chlorophyll c, carotenoids and xanthophylls.\nWhy (2) fits: their colour ranges from olive green to shades of brown depending on how much fucoxanthin, a xanthophyll, is present.\nWhy the others fail: r-phycoerythrin is the pigment of red algae. Chlorophyll b and chlorophyll d are not pigments of brown algae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Pyrenoids are storage bodies found in the chloroplasts of most green algae. What do they contain?","image":"","options":["Starch only","Oil droplets and starch","Protein besides starch","Floridean starch and protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the pyrenoids of green algae contain protein besides starch.\nSome green algae store oil droplets, but these are not part of the pyrenoid. So (2) is wrong.\nFloridean starch is the food reserve of red algae. So (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The plant body of a typical brown alga is attached to the substratum by a holdfast and has a stalk. What is its leaf-like photosynthetic organ called?","image":"","options":["Stipe","Frond","Thallus lobe","Rhizoid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the plant body of a brown alga has three parts. The holdfast attaches it to the substratum, the stipe is the stalk, and the frond is the leaf-like photosynthetic organ.\nWhy (2) fits: the frond is the part that carries out photosynthesis.\nWhy the tempting option fails: the stipe is only the stalk, not the photosynthetic organ. Rhizoids are not part of the brown algal body.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs consists only of haploid structures?","image":"","options":["Gemma of Marchantia and seta of Funaria","Capsule wall of a moss and protonema of a moss","Gemma of Marchantia and protonema of Funaria","Foot of a liverwort sporophyte and spore mother cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gametophyte structures are haploid ($n$). Sporophyte structures and spore mother cells are diploid ($2n$).\n(1) The seta of Funaria is sporophytic, so it is $2n$.\n(2) The capsule wall is sporophytic, so it is $2n$.\n(3) The gemma arises from the haploid gametophyte, and the protonema grows from a haploid spore. Both are $n$.\n(4) The foot is sporophytic, and the spore mother cell is $2n$ because it is the cell that undergoes meiosis.\nOnly pair (3) is entirely haploid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Species of the moss Sphagnum have long been used as packing material for trans-shipment of living material. This use is mainly due to their:","image":"","options":["high protein content","ability to fix atmospheric nitrogen","capacity to hold water","production of algin and agar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: Sphagnum (peat moss) can absorb and hold a large amount of water.\nBecause of this, it keeps living material moist during trans-shipment, which is why it is used for packing.\nOption (4) is the tempting distractor, but algin and agar come from brown and red algae, not mosses. Mosses do not fix nitrogen, and high protein content is not the reason for this use.\nSphagnum is also the source of peat, which is used as fuel.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about systems of plant classification is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Linnaeus's system was artificial, being based largely on vegetative characters or androecium structure.","Bentham and Hooker's classification of flowering plants was a natural system that also considered internal features.","Numerical taxonomy gives greater weightage to reproductive characters, since vegetative characters are easily affected by environment.","Cytotaxonomy uses information such as chromosome number, structure and behaviour."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Linnaeus's artificial system relied on vegetative characters or androecium structure.\n(2) Correct: Bentham and Hooker gave a natural system that considered both external and internal features.\n(3) Incorrect: numerical taxonomy codes all observable characters and gives each one equal importance. It does not weight reproductive characters more heavily.\n(4) Correct: cytotaxonomy uses chromosome number, structure and behaviour.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Archegonia occur in three of the following plant groups. Which group does NOT bear archegonia?","image":"","options":["Bryophytes","Pteridophytes","Gymnosperms","Angiosperms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Bryophytes and pteridophytes bear archegonia on their gametophytes.\nThe gymnosperm female gametophyte also bears two or more archegonia, even though gymnosperms produce seeds.\nIn angiosperms the female gametophyte is the embryo sac, which has an egg apparatus but no archegonia.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements comparing gymnosperms with heterosporous pteridophytes such as Selaginella is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Both produce two kinds of spores, microspores and megaspores","In both, the female gametophyte is retained on the parent sporophyte for some period","In gymnosperms, the pollen tube carries the male gametes towards the archegonia, so external water is not needed for their transfer","In gymnosperms, the male gametophyte leads an independent free-living life like the prothallus of ferns"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) True: both are heterosporous.\n(2) True: in both, the female gametophyte is retained on the parent sporophyte for some time.\n(3) True: in gymnosperms the pollen tube delivers the male gametes, so external water is not needed for their transfer.\n(4) False: gymnosperm gametophytes have no free-living existence. The male gametophyte is the reduced pollen grain, which forms inside the microsporangium. It is nothing like an independent fern prothallus.\nThe question asks for the incorrect statement, so (4) is the choice.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The giant Redwood tree (Sequoia sempervirens) is a/an","image":"","options":["Angiosperm","Tree fern","Pteridophyte","Gymnosperm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT names Sequoia, the giant redwood, as a gymnosperm and one of the tallest trees.\nIts ovules and seeds are not enclosed in an ovary. They are borne naked on cone scales, which is the defining feature of gymnosperms.\nOption (1), angiosperm, is tempting because it is a huge woody tree with seeds. But angiosperms enclose their seeds in fruits, which Sequoia lacks.\nIt is not a tree fern or a pteridophyte, because those plants reproduce by spores, not seeds.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Members of Chlorophyceae are usually grass green in colour. This is mainly due to the dominance of which pigments?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll a and c","Chlorophyll a and b","Chlorophyll a and d","Chlorophyll a and fucoxanthin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Chlorophyceae, chlorophyll a and b dominate. They are located in definite chloroplasts and give the grass-green colour.\nChlorophyll a and c with fucoxanthin give brown algae their colour.\nChlorophyll d occurs in red algae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Apart from vegetative characters, the artificial system of classification given by Linnaeus was based mainly on the structure of the:","image":"","options":["Gynoecium","Androecium","Embryo","Chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the artificial system of Linnaeus was based on vegetative characters or on the structure of the androecium (stamens).\nOption (1), gynoecium, is the tempting wrong answer, because it is also a floral organ. However, Linnaeus grouped plants by stamen number and arrangement, not by carpels.\nEmbryo and chromosome characters were used only later, in natural systems and in cytotaxonomy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In heterosporous pteridophytes such as Selaginella, the zygote develops into a young embryo within the female gametophyte","In gymnosperms, the multicellular female gametophyte bears two or more archegonia and is retained within the megasporangium","The male gametophyte of gymnosperms is highly reduced to a limited number of cells and is called the pollen grain","In gymnosperms, the megaspore mother cell differentiates from one of the cells of the integument surrounding the nucellus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in Selaginella, the zygote develops into a young embryo retained within the female gametophyte. This is a precursor to the seed habit.\n(2) Correct: the gymnosperm female gametophyte is multicellular, bears two or more archegonia and stays inside the megasporangium.\n(3) Correct: the male gametophyte is reduced to a few cells and is called the pollen grain.\n(4) Incorrect: the megaspore mother cell arises from a cell of the nucellus. The integuments only protect the nucellus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The sporophyte of a liverwort is differentiated into:","image":"","options":["Root, stem and leaves","Holdfast, stipe and frond","Foot, seta and capsule","Rhizoids, gemma cup and thallus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The liverwort sporophyte is differentiated into a foot, a seta and a capsule.\nMeiosis occurs in the capsule to produce spores.\nHoldfast, stipe and frond describe brown algae.\nRoot, stem and leaves describe the pteridophyte sporophyte.\nRhizoids, gemma cup and thallus are gametophyte features.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Artificial systems of classification are not acceptable, partly because they gave equal weightage to vegetative and sexual characteristics.\nReason (R): Vegetative characters are often more easily affected by the environment.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Artificial systems are not acceptable because they used only a few characters and gave equal weight to vegetative and sexual characteristics.\nR is true. Vegetative characters such as leaf size and habit are often easily changed by the environment.\nR explains A. Because vegetative traits are so variable, giving them the same weight as stable sexual traits produces misleading groupings.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about gymnosperms are correct?\n(i) They are heterosporous and produce haploid microspores and megaspores.\n(ii) Pollen grains develop within the microsporangia.\n(iii) The female gametophyte is shed from the ovule and lives independently in damp soil.\n(iv) The megaspore mother cell divides mitotically to form four megaspores.\n(v) Pollen grains are carried by air currents to the opening of the ovules.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: gymnosperms are heterosporous and produce haploid microspores and megaspores.\n(ii) Correct: pollen grains develop inside the microsporangia.\n(iii) Incorrect: the female gametophyte stays inside the megasporangium (ovule). It never lives free in soil.\n(iv) Incorrect: the megaspore mother cell divides by meiosis, not mitosis, to give four megaspores.\n(v) Correct: air currents carry pollen to the opening of the ovules.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The spread of living pteridophytes is limited and restricted to narrow geographical regions.\nReason (R): Pteridophytes lack xylem and phloem, so they cannot conduct water efficiently.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Living pteridophytes are limited to narrow geographical regions. Their gametophytes need cool, damp, shady places, and fertilisation needs water.\nR is false. Pteridophytes are the first terrestrial plants to have vascular tissues, xylem and phloem.\nSo the limited spread is due to dependence on water, not to lack of vascular tissue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following genera is a member of Rhodophyceae?","image":"","options":["Polysiphonia","Dictyota","Ectocarpus","Fucus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT examples of red algae (Rhodophyceae): Polysiphonia, Porphyra, Gracilaria and Gelidium.\nWhy (1) fits: Polysiphonia is on this list.\nWhy the others fail: Dictyota, Ectocarpus and Fucus are all brown algae (Phaeophyceae).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which group of plants is regarded, in evolutionary terms, as the first terrestrial plants to possess vascular tissues (xylem and phloem)?","image":"","options":["Bryophytes","Pteridophytes","Gymnosperms","Algae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: pteridophytes are the first terrestrial plants to possess vascular tissues, xylem and phloem.\nBryophytes are the tempting wrong option. They live on land but lack vascular tissues.\nGymnosperms do have xylem and phloem, but they evolved later than pteridophytes.\nAlgae are mostly aquatic and non-vascular.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In algae, vegetative reproduction occurs by","image":"","options":["formation of zoospores in zoosporangia","fusion of two similar gametes","budding of gemmae from gemma cups","fragmentation, in which each fragment grows into a thallus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT: algae reproduce vegetatively by fragmentation. Each fragment develops into a new thallus.\n(1) fails: zoospores formed in zoosporangia are a means of asexual reproduction.\n(2) fails: fusion of similar gametes is isogamy, a form of sexual reproduction.\n(3) fails: gemmae in gemma cups are found in liverworts (bryophytes), not algae.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Pinus, the male and the female cones are borne on the same tree.\nStatement II: In Cycas, the male cones and the megasporophylls are borne on different trees.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: Pinus is monoecious, so male and female cones grow on the same tree.\nStatement II is correct: Cycas is dioecious. Male cones grow on one plant, and megasporophylls grow on a separate plant. Cycas does not form a compact female cone; its megasporophylls are loosely clustered.\nSince both statements are true, option (3) fits.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\nI. In heterosporous pteridophytes, the zygote develops into a young embryo within the female gametophyte retained on the parent sporophyte, which is a precursor to the seed habit.\nII. In gymnosperms, the female gametophyte has an independent, free-living existence like the prothallus of ferns.\nIII. In Cycas, male cones and megasporophylls are borne on the same tree.\nIV. In gymnosperms, the megaspore mother cell differentiates from one of the cells of the nucellus.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["I and IV only","I, II and IV only","II and III only","I, III and IV only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(I) Correct: in heterosporous pteridophytes (e.g., Selaginella, Salvinia), the female gametophyte stays on the parent sporophyte for a variable period. The zygote develops into a young embryo inside it, which NCERT calls a precursor to the seed habit.\n(II) Incorrect: in gymnosperms, neither gametophyte is free-living. Both remain within sporangia on the sporophyte, unlike the independent fern prothallus.\n(III) Incorrect: Cycas is dioecious, so male cones and megasporophylls are borne on different trees. Pinus bears both on the same tree.\n(IV) Correct: the megaspore mother cell differentiates from one cell of the nucellus inside the ovule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Cyanobacteria are classified under","image":"","options":["Protista","Plantae","Monera","Algae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae) are prokaryotes. They lack a membrane-bound nucleus and organelles.\nIn Whittaker's five-kingdom classification, all prokaryotes are placed in Kingdom Monera.\nThe name 'algae' is tempting, but Algae is not a kingdom. True algae are eukaryotes, placed in Protista or Plantae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match the plant in List-I with the site where meiosis occurs in List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Liverwort\n(b) Fern\n(c) Gymnosperm\n(d) Angiosperm\nList-II:\n(i) Anthers and ovules within flowers\n(ii) Capsule of the sporophyte attached to the gametophyte\n(iii) Microsporangia and megasporangia borne in cones\n(iv) Sporangia borne on the leaves of the free-living sporophyte","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Meiosis always occurs in the sporophyte, inside sporangia, to form spores.\n(a) Liverwort: the sporophyte capsule stays attached to the gametophyte, so a-ii.\n(b) Fern: sporangia are borne on the leaves of the free-living sporophyte, so b-iv.\n(c) Gymnosperm: microsporangia and megasporangia are borne in cones, so c-iii.\n(d) Angiosperm: meiosis occurs in the anthers and ovules of flowers, so d-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about heterospory and the seed habit is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In Selaginella, the female gametophyte is retained on the parent sporophyte for variable periods, and the young embryo develops within it","In gymnosperms, the pollen grain is a highly reduced male gametophyte that develops within the microsporangium","In Cycas, male cones and megasporophylls are borne on the same tree, as in Pinus","In gymnosperms, one megaspore develops into a multicellular female gametophyte bearing two or more archegonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in heterosporous Selaginella, the female gametophyte is retained on the sporophyte and the embryo develops inside it. This is a precursor to the seed habit.\n(2) Correct: the pollen grain is the reduced male gametophyte and develops in the microsporangium.\n(3) Incorrect: Pinus bears male and female cones on the same tree, but Cycas is dioecious. Its male cones and megasporophylls are on different trees.\n(4) Correct: a single functional megaspore forms the multicellular female gametophyte, which bears two or more archegonia.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Holdfast, stipe and frond constitutes the plant body in case of","image":"","options":["Rhodophyceae","Chlorophyceae","Phaeophyceae","All of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In brown algae (Phaeophyceae), the plant body is usually differentiated into three parts:\na holdfast for attachment, a stalk-like stipe, and a leaf-like photosynthetic frond. Laminaria and Fucus are examples.\nRed and green algae do not typically show this three-part differentiation, so 'All of the above' is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding classification systems is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Phylogenetic systems assume that organisms of the same taxon have a common ancestor","Information from other sources becomes especially important when there is no supporting fossil evidence","Bentham and Hooker's classification is a phylogenetic system based on evolutionary relationships","Artificial systems separated closely related species because they were based on a few characteristics"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: phylogenetic systems assume that organisms of the same taxon share a common ancestor.\n(2) Correct: data from other sources becomes especially important when no fossil evidence is available.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT describes Bentham and Hooker's classification as a natural system based on natural affinities. It is not a phylogenetic system.\n(4) Correct: artificial systems used only a few superficial characters, so closely related species were often separated.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly traces the life cycle of a moss from the first cell of the gametophytic generation to the first cell of the next sporophytic generation?","image":"","options":["Spore mother cell → protonema → leafy stage → zygote","Spore → protonema → leafy stage bearing antheridia and archegonia → zygote","Spore → leafy stage → protonema bearing sex organs → zygote","Spore → protonema → leafy stage → capsule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The first cell of the moss gametophyte is the haploid spore.\nThe spore germinates into the creeping, filamentous protonema.\nLateral buds on the protonema form the leafy stage, which bears antheridia and archegonia.\nFertilisation gives the diploid zygote, the first cell of the new sporophyte.\nOption (1) is wrong because the spore mother cell is 2n and sporophytic. Option (3) reverses the protonema and leafy stage. Option (4) ends at the capsule, which is not the first sporophytic cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure) with List-II (its nature).\nList-I\nA. Protonema of moss\nB. Seta of moss\nC. Prothallus of fern\nD. Pollen grain of Pinus\nList-II\n(i) Diploid; part of a sporophyte that depends on the gametophyte for nourishment\n(ii) Haploid; free-living, mostly photosynthetic thalloid gametophyte\n(iii) Haploid; highly reduced male gametophyte confined to a few cells\n(iv) Haploid; creeping, filamentous stage that develops directly from a spore\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Protonema: the haploid, creeping, filamentous first stage arising from a spore → (iv).\nB. Seta: part of the diploid moss sporophyte (foot, seta, capsule), nourished by the gametophyte → (i).\nC. Fern prothallus: a free-living, photosynthetic, thalloid haploid gametophyte → (ii).\nD. Pinus pollen grain: a highly reduced haploid male gametophyte → (iii).\nNot every moss part is haploid; the seta is diploid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following pteridophytes produces two kinds of spores, microspores and megaspores?","image":"","options":["Dryopteris","Salvinia","Pteris","Adiantum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Most pteridophytes are homosporous and produce only one kind of spore.\nNCERT names Selaginella and Salvinia as heterosporous genera. They produce small microspores and large megaspores.\nDryopteris, Pteris and Adiantum are ferns of Pteropsida, and all are homosporous.\nSo Salvinia is the only option that produces two kinds of spores.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about green algae.\nStatement I: Green algae usually have a rigid cell wall made of an inner layer of pectose and an outer layer of cellulose.\nStatement II: In green algae, the pigments are localised in definite chloroplasts.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The layers are reversed: the green algal cell wall has an inner layer of cellulose and an outer layer of pectose.\nStatement II is correct. In green algae the pigments are localised in definite chloroplasts, which may be discoid, plate-like, reticulate, cup-shaped, spiral or ribbon-shaped.\nSo only Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct for mosses?\n(i) The protonema develops directly from a spore and is creeping, green, branched and frequently filamentous.\n(ii) Antheridia and archegonia are borne on the protonema.\n(iii) Vegetative reproduction occurs by fragmentation and by budding in the secondary protonema.\n(iv) The sporophyte is less elaborate than that of liverworts.\n(v) Spores are formed in the capsule after meiosis.\n(vi) Gemmae formed in gemma cups are their main means of asexual reproduction.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the protonema develops directly from the spore and is creeping, green, branched and often filamentous.\n(ii) Incorrect: sex organs are borne at the apex of the leafy shoots, not on the protonema.\n(iii) Correct: mosses reproduce vegetatively by fragmentation and by budding in the secondary protonema.\n(iv) Incorrect: the moss sporophyte is more elaborate than the liverwort sporophyte.\n(v) Correct: spores form in the capsule after meiosis.\n(vi) Incorrect: gemma cups are typical of liverworts.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In numerical taxonomy, each character is given equal importance.\nStatement II: Numerical taxonomy can handle only a few characters at a time, so it is usually done manually.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Numerical taxonomy codes all observable characters with numbers and gives each character equal importance.\nStatement II is incorrect. Numerical taxonomy is carried out on computers, so hundreds of characters can be considered at the same time. It is not limited to a few characters, and it is not done manually.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Red algae occur both in well-lighted regions close to the water surface and at great depths in the ocean.\nReason (R): Red algae lack chlorophyll a and depend entirely on r-phycoerythrin for photosynthesis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that red algae occur both in well-lighted regions near the water surface and at great depths in the ocean, where little light penetrates.\nR is false. Rhodophyceae do contain chlorophyll a, along with chlorophyll d and r-phycoerythrin. r-phycoerythrin is the pigment that gives them their red colour.\nSince R is false, it cannot explain A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In bryophytes, the zygote undergoes reduction division immediately after fertilisation to produce haploid spores.\nStatement II: In bryophytes, haploid spores are produced by meiosis in some cells of the multicellular sporophyte.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The bryophyte zygote does not undergo reduction division right away. It first develops into a multicellular sporophyte.\nStatement II is correct. Only some cells of the sporophyte undergo meiosis to produce haploid spores.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups consists only of members of Chlorophyceae?","image":"","options":["Chlamydomonas, Chara, Spirogyra","Volvox, Fucus, Ulothrix","Spirogyra, Porphyra, Chara","Ulothrix, Dictyota, Volvox"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Chlorophyceae are the green algae. Examples include Chlamydomonas, Volvox, Ulothrix, Spirogyra and Chara.\n(1) Correct: Chlamydomonas, Chara and Spirogyra are all green algae.\n(2) Incorrect: Fucus is a brown alga.\n(3) Incorrect: Porphyra is a red alga.\n(4) Incorrect: Dictyota is a brown alga.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following structures in a gymnosperm such as Pinus is haploid?","image":"","options":["Nucellus of the ovule","Cells of the megasporophyll","Cells of the female gametophyte that bears the archegonia","Embryo formed after fertilisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The female gametophyte develops from a megaspore formed by meiosis, so its cells (including those bearing archegonia) are haploid ($n$).\nThe nucellus and megasporophyll are parts of the diploid ($2n$) sporophyte.\nThe embryo develops from the zygote formed by fertilisation, so it is also diploid. Do not confuse it with the gametophyte.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cyanobacteria, though called blue green algae, are no longer placed among the algae of Kingdom Plantae.\nStatement II: In Spirogyra, the gametes are flagellated and similar in size.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Cyanobacteria are prokaryotes placed in Monera, so they are no longer treated as algae of Plantae.\nStatement II is incorrect. Spirogyra is isogamous, but its gametes are non-flagellated (non-motile).\nFlagellated isogametes are seen in Ulothrix and Chlamydomonas, not Spirogyra.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following genera belongs to Rhodophyceae?","image":"","options":["Dictyota","Sargassum","Gracilaria","Ectocarpus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Rhodophyceae are the red algae. NCERT examples are Polysiphonia, Porphyra, Gracilaria and Gelidium.\nGracilaria is a red alga, so it fits.\nDictyota, Sargassum and Ectocarpus are all Phaeophyceae (brown algae). Sargassum is the most tempting distractor because it is a well-known marine seaweed, but it is brown.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about mosses is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The protonema develops directly from a spore and is a creeping, green, branched, frequently filamentous stage","The leafy stage develops from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud","Antheridia and archegonia are produced at the apex of the leafy shoots","The sporophyte of mosses is simpler than that of liverworts and lacks an elaborate spore dispersal mechanism"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the protonema develops directly from a spore. It is creeping, green, branched and often filamentous.\n(2) Correct: the leafy stage arises from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud.\n(3) Correct: antheridia and archegonia form at the apex of the leafy shoots.\n(4) Incorrect: NCERT states the moss sporophyte (foot, seta, capsule) is more elaborate than the liverwort sporophyte. Mosses also have an elaborate spore-dispersal mechanism.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about members of Chlorophyceae are correct?\n(a) Their grass-green colour is due to the dominance of chlorophyll a and b.\n(b) Their plant body is always multicellular, either colonial or filamentous.\n(c) Asexual reproduction is by flagellated zoospores produced in zoosporangia.\n(d) Some members store food in the form of oil droplets.\n(e) Their motile cells bear two unequal, laterally inserted flagella.\n(f) Their reserve food is floridean starch.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: chlorophyll a and b dominate, giving the grass-green colour.\n(b) Incorrect: unicellular forms such as Chlamydomonas also occur.\n(c) Correct: asexual reproduction is by flagellated zoospores formed in zoosporangia.\n(d) Correct: some members store food as oil droplets.\n(e) Incorrect: green algae have 2–8 equal, apical flagella. Two unequal, lateral flagella are a feature of brown algae.\n(f) Incorrect: floridean starch is the reserve food of red algae.\nCorrect statements are (a), (c) and (d), so the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are included in NCERT's general description of algae?\nChlorophyll-bearing; thalloid body; autotrophic nutrition; largely aquatic; presence of xylem and phloem; differentiation into true root, stem and leaves","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines algae as chlorophyll-bearing, simple, thalloid, autotrophic and largely aquatic organisms.\nIncluded: chlorophyll-bearing, thalloid body, autotrophic nutrition, largely aquatic. That makes 4.\nExcluded: xylem and phloem, and true roots, stems and leaves. Vascular tissue first appears in pteridophytes, and algae lack true organs.\nSo 4 features apply.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Conifers are able to limit water loss even in conditions of extreme temperature, humidity and wind.\nReason (R): Conifers have needle-like leaves with reduced surface area, a thick cuticle and sunken stomata.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Conifers can withstand extremes of temperature, humidity and wind while limiting water loss.\nR is true. Their needle-like leaves reduce the surface area for transpiration. A thick cuticle and sunken stomata further reduce water loss.\nR correctly explains A, because these leaf adaptations are exactly what conserve water under harsh conditions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cells are haploid?\nProtonemal cell of a moss; leaf cell of a moss; prothallus cell of a fern; gemma cell of Marchantia; zygote of a fern; meristem cell of a monocot; ovum of a liverwort; primary endosperm nucleus of a dicot","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gametophyte cells are haploid (n). Sporophyte cells are diploid (2n).\nHaploid: moss protonema, moss leaf, fern prothallus, Marchantia gemma and the liverwort ovum. All belong to the gametophyte, which gives 5.\nNot haploid: the fern zygote and the monocot meristem cell are 2n. The primary endosperm nucleus is 3n, because it forms from fusion of a male gamete with the two polar nuclei.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In most brown algae, asexual reproduction is by pear-shaped zoospores bearing two unequal, laterally attached flagella.\nStatement II: Red algae reproduce asexually by non-motile spores.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT states that most brown algae reproduce asexually by biflagellate, pear-shaped (pyriform) zoospores. Their two flagella are unequal and laterally attached.\nStatement II: Correct. Red algae have no flagellated stages at all. They multiply asexually by non-motile spores and also reproduce vegetatively by fragmentation.\nBoth statements are true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Equisetum (horsetail) is placed in which class of pteridophytes?","image":"","options":["Psilopsida","Lycopsida","Pteropsida","Sphenopsida"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: pteridophytes are divided into Psilopsida (Psilotum), Lycopsida (Selaginella, Lycopodium), Sphenopsida (Equisetum) and Pteropsida (ferns such as Dryopteris, Pteris and Adiantum).\nEquisetum (horsetail) is the standard example of Sphenopsida.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a moss?","image":"","options":["Marchantia","Polytrichum","Selaginella","Ulothrix"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: mosses include Funaria, Polytrichum and Sphagnum (NCERT).\n(1) Marchantia is a thalloid liverwort, not a moss. It is the most tempting wrong option because it is also a bryophyte.\n(3) Selaginella is a pteridophyte.\n(4) Ulothrix is a filamentous green alga.\nSo Polytrichum is the moss.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Among the three classes of algae (Chlorophyceae, Phaeophyceae, Rhodophyceae), which of the following characters is found only in Chlorophyceae?","image":"","options":["Presence of cellulose in the cell wall","Vegetative reproduction by fragmentation","Presence of chlorophyll b, with food stored as starch","Occurrence of oogamous sexual reproduction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll b, together with starch as the food reserve, is found only in green algae.\nBrown algae have chlorophyll a and c and store laminarin or mannitol.\nRed algae have chlorophyll a and d and store floridean starch.\nCellulose occurs in the walls of all three classes, so option (1) is shared.\nFragmentation occurs in all three classes, so option (2) is shared.\nOogamy occurs in all three as well (Volvox, Fucus, red algae), so option (4) is not unique.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In Marchantia, gemmae are best described as:","image":"","options":["Haploid non-motile spores formed by meiosis inside the capsule","Green, multicellular asexual buds formed in small receptacles on the thallus","Diploid buds formed at the tip of the seta","Flagellated male gametes released from the antheridia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Gemmae are green, multicellular asexual buds. They develop in small receptacles called gemma cups on the Marchantia thallus.\nThey detach from the parent and grow into new individuals.\nThey are not spores, because they are not formed by meiosis.\nThey are not diploid, and they are not motile gametes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a typical bryophyte:\nList-I\n(a) Rhizoids\n(b) Archegonium\n(c) Antheridium\n(d) Sporophyte\nList-II\n(i) Produces biflagellate male gametes\n(ii) Some cells undergo meiosis to form haploid spores\n(iii) Attach the plant body to the substratum\n(iv) Flask-shaped organ producing a single egg","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Rhizoids anchor the plant body to the substratum, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) The archegonium is flask-shaped and produces a single egg, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) The antheridium produces biflagellate antherozoids, so (c)-(i).\n(d) In the sporophyte, some cells undergo meiosis to form haploid spores, so (d)-(ii).\nOption (2) swaps the two sex organs, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the economic importance of algae are correct?\n(i) At least half of the total carbon dioxide fixation on earth is carried out by algae.\n(ii) Algin is a hydrocolloid produced in large amounts by red algae.\n(iii) Carrageen is a water-holding substance obtained from red algae.\n(iv) Chlorella, a multicellular brown alga, is used as a protein-rich food supplement by space travellers.\n(v) Porphyra, Laminaria and Sargassum are among the 70 species of marine algae used as food.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: algae carry out at least half of the earth's total CO$_2$ fixation.\n(ii) Wrong: algin comes from brown algae.\n(iii) Correct: carrageen comes from red algae.\n(iv) Wrong: Chlorella is a unicellular alga, not a multicellular brown alga.\n(v) Correct: Porphyra, Laminaria and Sargassum are among the 70 species of marine algae used as food.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about bryophytes are correct?\n(i) The main plant body is haploid and produces gametes.\n(ii) The antheridium produces biflagellate antherozoids.\n(iii) The flask-shaped archegonium produces numerous eggs.\n(iv) The sex organs are unicellular.\n(v) Haploid spores are formed by meiosis in some cells of the sporophyte.\n(vi) The plant body is anchored by true roots in the leafy forms.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the main plant body is the haploid gametophyte, which produces gametes.\n(ii) Correct: the antheridium produces biflagellate antherozoids.\n(iii) Incorrect: the flask-shaped archegonium produces a single egg.\n(iv) Incorrect: the sex organs are multicellular.\n(v) Correct: some sporophyte cells undergo meiosis to form haploid spores.\n(vi) Incorrect: bryophytes have no true roots and are anchored by rhizoids.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Phylogenetic systems of classification, which are accepted at present, are based on:","image":"","options":["Gross morphological characters such as habit and leaf shape","Evolutionary relationships between organisms","Only the chemical constituents of plants","Only the number of stamens in a flower"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: phylogenetic systems, which are accepted today, are based on evolutionary relationships. They assume that members of a taxon share a common ancestor.\nOption (1) describes artificial systems, which relied on gross superficial morphology such as habit and leaf shape.\nUsing only chemical constituents (chemotaxonomy) or only stamen number gives just a single line of evidence. Neither is the basis of phylogenetic classification.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Phaeophyceae is incorrect?","image":"","options":["They possess chlorophyll a and c along with carotenoids and xanthophylls","Their vegetative cells have a cellulosic wall usually covered on the outside by a gelatinous coating of algin","Their gametes are pyriform with two laterally attached flagella, and gametic union always takes place in the surrounding water","Besides plastids, their protoplast contains a centrally located vacuole and nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: brown algae have chlorophyll a and c, carotenoids and xanthophylls.\n(2) Correct: the cellulosic wall is usually covered outside by a gelatinous coating of algin.\n(3) Incorrect: the gametes are pyriform with two lateral flagella, but union may occur in water or within the oogonium in oogamous species. The word 'always' makes it wrong.\n(4) Correct: the protoplast has plastids, a centrally located vacuole and a nucleus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Sexual reproduction in Spirogyra is described as isogamous even though its gametes are non-motile.\nReason (R): Isogamy refers to the fusion of gametes that are similar in size, whether or not they are flagellated.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Spirogyra's gametes are non-flagellated, yet its sexual reproduction is called isogamous.\nR is true: NCERT defines isogamy as the fusion of gametes similar in size. These may be flagellated (Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (Spirogyra).\nIsogamy depends on size, not motility, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Carrageen is a commercially used hydrocolloid (water-holding substance). Which group of algae produces it in large amounts?","image":"","options":["Red algae","Brown algae","Green algae","Blue-green algae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Hydrocolloids are substances that hold water, and they are used commercially.\nRed algae produce carrageen in large amounts.\nBrown algae produce algin. Swapping these two hydrocolloids is the usual trap.\nGreen algae and blue-green algae are not the commercial source of carrageen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The prothallus of a pteridophyte is best described as:","image":"","options":["A diploid, dependent sporophyte attached to the gametophyte","A compact cone of sporophylls bearing sporangia","A haploid, free-living, mostly photosynthetic, thalloid gametophyte","A diploid embryo retained within the female gametophyte"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: pteridophyte spores germinate into the prothallus.\nThe prothallus is a small but multicellular, free-living, mostly photosynthetic, thalloid gametophyte. Because it develops from a haploid spore, it is haploid.\nOption (2) describes a strobilus (cone of sporophylls), not a prothallus.\nOptions (1) and (4) describe diploid structures, but the prothallus is haploid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The most common type of spore produced during asexual reproduction in algae is the","image":"","options":["aplanospore, which is non-motile","zoospore, which is flagellated","conidium, which is exogenous","oospore, which is thick-walled"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Algae reproduce asexually by different types of spores.\nNCERT states that the most common of these are zoospores. Zoospores are flagellated (motile) and germinate to give rise to new plants.\nConidia are exogenous spores of fungi, not algae.\nAn oospore is formed after sexual fusion, so it is not an asexual spore.\nAplanospores are non-motile and are not the most common type.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Pyrenoids are storage bodies found in the chloroplasts of most members of Chlorophyceae. Besides starch, pyrenoids contain:","image":"","options":["Protein","Floridean starch","Laminarin","Mannitol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in Chlorophyceae (green algae), pyrenoids are storage bodies inside the chloroplasts. They contain protein as well as starch.\nWhy (1) fits: protein is the only option that is stored alongside starch in green algal pyrenoids.\nWhy the others fail: laminarin and mannitol are the reserve foods of brown algae. Floridean starch is the reserve food of red algae.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Members of Phaeophyceae range in colour from olive green to various shades of brown.\nReason (R): The colour of brown algae depends on the amount of the xanthophyll pigment fucoxanthin present in them.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: brown algae vary in colour from olive green to various shades of brown.\nR is true: they contain chlorophyll a and c, carotenoids and the xanthophyll fucoxanthin.\nR explains A. NCERT states that the colour depends on the amount of fucoxanthin present. More fucoxanthin masks the chlorophyll more, giving a browner shade.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Protonema is","image":"","options":["Haploid and is found in mosses","Diploid and is found in liverworts","Diploid and is found in pteridophytes","Haploid and is found in pteridophytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: in mosses, a haploid spore germinates into the protonema. This creeping, green, branched filament is the first stage of the gametophyte.\nIt grows from a haploid (n) spore by mitosis, so it is haploid. This rules out options (2) and (3), which say diploid.\nOption (4) fails because pteridophytes have a prothallus, not a protonema. Liverworts also lack a distinct protonema stage.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Algin\n(b) Carrageen\n(c) Agar\n(d) Chlorella\nList-II\n(i) Protein-rich food supplement\n(ii) Hydrocolloid from red algae\n(iii) Hydrocolloid from brown algae\n(iv) Used to grow microbes; obtained from Gelidium\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Algin is the hydrocolloid from brown algae, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Carrageen is the hydrocolloid from red algae, so (b)-(ii).\n(c) Agar is obtained from Gelidium and used to grow microbes, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) Chlorella is a protein-rich food supplement, so (d)-(i).\nOption 1 swaps algin and carrageen. Options 2 and 4 misplace agar and Chlorella.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A plant shows the following features:\n(i) a dominant sporophyte with true roots, stem and leaves containing xylem and phloem\n(ii) small leaves (microphylls)\n(iii) sporophylls grouped into compact strobili\n(iv) two kinds of spores, with the female gametophyte retained on the parent sporophyte\n(v) no ovules\nThe plant is most likely:","image":"","options":["Equisetum","Selaginella","Cycas","Dryopteris"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Clues (i)–(iii) point to a pteridophyte with microphylls and strobili. A dominant vascular sporophyte with small leaves and compact strobili matches a lycopod.\nClue (iv) narrows it down. Heterospory with the female gametophyte retained on the sporophyte is seen in Selaginella.\nClue (v) rules out Cycas, which is a gymnosperm and bears ovules.\nEquisetum has strobili but is homosporous.\nDryopteris is a homosporous fern with large leaves (macrophylls).\nSo the plant is Selaginella.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Living pteridophytes are restricted to narrow geographical regions.\nReason (R): Their gametophytes need cool, damp, shady places to grow, and water is required for fertilisation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that living pteridophytes are limited in spread and restricted to narrow geographical regions.\nR is true. The gametophyte (prothallus) needs cool, damp, shady places to grow. Fertilisation also needs water, because antherozoids must swim to the archegonium.\nR explains A. These two requirements limit where the plants can complete their life cycle, so their spread is restricted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bryophytes are called the amphibians of the plant kingdom.\nReason (R): Bryophytes can live in soil, but they depend on water for sexual reproduction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Bryophytes are called the amphibians of the plant kingdom.\nR is true. They live in soil, but water is needed for sexual reproduction, because the antherozoids swim to the archegonium.\nThis dependence on both land and water is exactly why they are called amphibians, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about mosses is correct?","image":"","options":["The leafy stage is anchored by unicellular, unbranched rhizoids","Vegetative reproduction occurs by fragmentation and by budding in the secondary protonema","Antheridia and archegonia are produced at the base of the leafy shoots","The sporophyte is the predominant stage of the life cycle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: moss rhizoids are multicellular and branched. Unicellular rhizoids are found in liverworts.\n(2) Correct: NCERT states that mosses reproduce vegetatively by fragmentation and by budding in the secondary protonema.\n(3) Incorrect: antheridia and archegonia form at the apex of the leafy shoots, not at the base.\n(4) Incorrect: the haploid gametophyte is the predominant stage in mosses. The sporophyte depends on it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about pteridophytes is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Sporangia are subtended by leaf-like appendages called sporophylls","Spores are produced by meiosis in spore mother cells within the sporangia","In heterosporous forms, the zygote develops into a young embryo within the female gametophyte, which is considered a precursor to the seed habit","The sporophyte remains attached to the gametophyte and derives nourishment from it throughout its life"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Sporangia are subtended by leaf-like sporophylls.\n(2) Correct. Spore mother cells in the sporangia undergo meiosis to form spores.\n(3) Correct. In heterosporous forms the embryo develops within the retained female gametophyte. This is considered a precursor to the seed habit.\n(4) Incorrect. In pteridophytes the sporophyte is the dominant, independent phase. A sporophyte that stays attached to the gametophyte and depends on it is a feature of bryophytes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The natural system of classification for flowering plants, which used internal features as well as external ones, was given by:","image":"","options":["Carolus Linnaeus","R.H. Whittaker","George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker","Engler and Prantl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT credits George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker with a natural system for flowering plants. It was based on natural affinities and used internal features (ultrastructure, anatomy, embryology, phytochemistry) as well as external ones.\nOption (1) fails because Linnaeus gave an artificial system based on a few characters, mainly the androecium.\nWhittaker proposed the five kingdom classification of all living organisms, not a system for flowering plants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"If the diploid number of a flowering plant is 36. What would be the chromosome number in its endosperm?","image":"","options":["36","18","54","72"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: diploid number $2n = 36$, so $n = 18$.\nFormula: endosperm $= 3n$. It is triploid because one male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei (triple fusion).\nSubstituting: $3 \\times 18 = 54$.\nSo the endosperm has 54 chromosomes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A marine alga has these features:\n- No flagellated cell appears at any stage of its life.\n- It stores a carbohydrate very similar in structure to amylopectin and glycogen.\n- Its cell wall contains polysulphate esters.\nWhich option correctly identifies a possible genus and a commercial use associated with it?","image":"","options":["Laminaria – source of algin","Gelidium – source of agar used to grow microbes","Fucus – oogamous alga with a large non-motile egg, used for agar","Chlamydomonas – protein-rich food supplement"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Clues: no flagellated stage, stored food like amylopectin and glycogen (floridean starch), and polysulphate esters in the wall. All three point to red algae (Rhodophyceae).\nGelidium is a red alga, and agar from it is used to grow microbes. This fits.\nLaminaria and Fucus are brown algae with flagellated zoospores or gametes. Fucus is also not an agar source.\nChlamydomonas is a flagellated green alga.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In bryophytes, the sporophyte is not free-living and remains attached to the gametophyte.\nReason (R): In bryophytes, the zygote undergoes reduction division immediately after fertilisation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The bryophyte sporophyte is attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and draws nourishment from it.\nR is false. The zygote does not undergo meiosis immediately. It first develops into a multicellular sporophyte.\nOnly some cells of the sporophyte later undergo meiosis to form haploid spores.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly contrasts liverworts with mosses?","image":"","options":["Liverworts: gametophyte arises from a protonema bearing lateral buds; Mosses: gametophyte grows directly from the spore as a flat thallus","Liverworts: leafy members bear tiny leaf-like appendages in two rows; Mosses: upright axes bear spirally arranged leaves","Liverworts: sporophyte has foot, seta and capsule; Mosses: sporophyte lacks a seta","Liverworts: meiosis occurs inside the gemma cup; Mosses: meiosis occurs inside the antheridium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the features are reversed. The protonema with lateral buds belongs to mosses, and the flat thallus belongs to thalloid liverworts.\n(2) Correct: leafy liverworts bear tiny leaf-like appendages in two rows. Moss upright axes bear spirally arranged leaves.\n(3) Incorrect: the sporophytes of both groups have a foot, seta and capsule.\n(4) Incorrect: in both groups meiosis occurs in the capsule, not in the gemma cup or antheridium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about pteridophytes are correct?\n(i) The main plant body is a sporophyte differentiated into true root, stem and leaves.\n(ii) Spores are produced by mitosis in spore mother cells.\n(iii) The gametophyte is multicellular, free-living and mostly photosynthetic.\n(iv) Antherozoids reach the archegonium by air currents.\n(v) The zygote develops into a well-differentiated sporophyte, which is the dominant phase.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. The main plant body is a sporophyte with true root, stem and leaves.\n(ii) Incorrect. Spore mother cells form spores by meiosis, not mitosis.\n(iii) Correct. The gametophyte is multicellular, free-living and mostly photosynthetic.\n(iv) Incorrect. Water, not air, carries antherozoids to the mouth of the archegonium.\n(v) Correct. The zygote develops into the dominant, well-differentiated sporophyte.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Chlorella is used as a food supplement, even by space travellers. Which property of this alga makes it suitable for this use?","image":"","options":["It is a large kelp with a massive plant body","It is rich in agar","It produces carrageen in large amounts","It is a unicellular alga rich in proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Chlorella is a unicellular green alga that is rich in proteins.\nThis high protein content is why it is used as a food supplement, even by space travellers.\nIt is not a kelp. Kelps are large brown algae with massive bodies.\nAgar and carrageen both come from red algae, not from Chlorella.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are haploid?\n(i) Protonema of a moss\n(ii) Seta of Funaria\n(iii) Gemma of Marchantia\n(iv) Prothallus of a fern\n(v) Pollen grain of Pinus\n(vi) Nucellus in an ovule of Cycas\n(vii) Female gametophyte of Pinus\n(viii) Capsule wall of a liverwort sporophyte","image":"","options":["4","5","6","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Gametophytic structures are haploid (n), and sporophytic structures are diploid (2n).\n(i) Protonema is haploid, as it is the juvenile gametophyte.\n(ii) Seta is diploid, as it is part of the sporophyte.\n(iii) Gemma is haploid, as it is an asexual bud of the gametophyte.\n(iv) Prothallus is haploid, as it is the fern gametophyte.\n(v) Pollen grain is haploid, as it is the reduced male gametophyte.\n(vi) Nucellus is diploid, as it is megasporangium tissue.\n(vii) Female gametophyte is haploid.\n(viii) Capsule wall is diploid, as it belongs to the sporophyte.\nTotal haploid = 5.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following algae has a colonial plant body?","image":"","options":["Ulothrix","Spirogyra","Volvox","Laminaria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes three forms of algal body: unicellular, colonial and filamentous, with some marine forms becoming massive.\nVolvox is the standard example of a colonial alga. Its many cells are arranged together in a hollow sphere.\nUlothrix and Spirogyra are filamentous green algae, so they are the tempting wrong choices.\nLaminaria is a kelp-type brown alga with a large, massive plant body, not a colony.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair correctly gives the smallest and one of the tallest angiosperms mentioned in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Sequoia and Eucalyptus","Wolffia and Sequoia","Wolffia and Eucalyptus","Chlorella and Eucalyptus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that angiosperms range from the tiny Wolffia to tall Eucalyptus trees (over 100 m).\nSequoia is tall but is a gymnosperm, so options (1) and (2) fail.\nChlorella is a unicellular green alga, not an angiosperm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In Cycas, the small specialised coralloid roots are associated with which of the following?","image":"","options":["Mycorrhizal fungi","Nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria","Root-nodule bacteria such as Rhizobium","Red algae living symbiotically"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: In Cycas, small specialised roots called coralloid roots are associated with nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria.\nThe tempting wrong option is mycorrhizal fungi. That fungal root association is NCERT's example for Pinus, not Cycas.\nRhizobium forms root nodules in legumes, and red algae are not root symbionts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A taxonomist cannot decide whether two plant populations belong to the same species. She compares their chromosome number, chromosome structure and chromosome behaviour during cell division. Which approach to classification is she using?","image":"","options":["Chemotaxonomy","Cytotaxonomy","Numerical taxonomy","Artificial classification"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cytotaxonomy uses cytological information to resolve taxonomic confusion. This includes chromosome number, chromosome structure and chromosome behaviour.\nThe taxonomist is comparing exactly these chromosome features, so she is using cytotaxonomy.\nChemotaxonomy is the tempting wrong choice, but it relies on the plant's chemical constituents, not its chromosomes.\nNumerical taxonomy codes characters for computer analysis, and artificial classification uses a few superficial characters.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In pteridophytes, the dominant phase of the life cycle is the diploid sporophyte, which is differentiated into true root, stem and leaves.\nStatement II: In gymnosperms, the male and female gametophytes are small but free-living, like the prothallus of ferns.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In pteridophytes the main plant body is the diploid sporophyte. It is differentiated into true root, stem and leaves, with vascular tissue.\nStatement II is incorrect. Gymnosperm male and female gametophytes have no independent free-living existence. They stay within the sporangia on the sporophyte. This differs from the free-living fern prothallus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Fusion of two motile gametes which are dissimilar in size is termed as","image":"","options":["Oogamy","Isogamy","Anisogamy","Zoogamy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Isogamy is the fusion of gametes that are similar in size.\nAnisogamy is the fusion of two gametes that differ in size. Both gametes may be motile, as in some Chlamydomonas species.\nOogamy also involves unequal gametes, but the large female gamete is non-motile. The question says both gametes are motile, so oogamy does not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Agar is used to grow microbes in the laboratory and to make ice-creams and jellies. From which pair of algae is it commercially obtained?","image":"","options":["Laminaria and Sargassum","Gelidium and Gracilaria","Ulothrix and Volvox","Fucus and Dictyota"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Agar is a commercial product of red algae. Its main sources are Gelidium and Gracilaria.\nIt is used as a culture medium for growing microbes and in ice-creams and jellies.\nLaminaria, Sargassum, Fucus and Dictyota are brown algae, which yield algin, not agar.\nUlothrix and Volvox are green algae and are not sources of agar.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In some pteridophytes, the female gametophyte is retained on the parent sporophyte for variable periods, and the zygote develops into a young embryo within it; this is regarded as a precursor to the seed habit.\nStatement II: This retention of the female gametophyte is characteristic of homosporous ferns such as Dryopteris.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In NCERT, heterosporous pteridophytes such as Selaginella and Salvinia produce megaspores that develop into female gametophytes. These gametophytes stay on the parent sporophyte for variable periods, and the zygote grows into a young embryo inside them. This is treated as a precursor to the seed habit.\nStatement II is incorrect. Dryopteris is homosporous. Its spores give rise to free-living, photosynthetic prothalli, so no female gametophyte is retained on the sporophyte.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In the ovule of a gymnosperm, the megaspore mother cell differentiates from which tissue?","image":"","options":["A cell of the nucellus","A cell of the protective envelope (integument)","A cell of the pollen tube","A cell of the microsporangium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: The gymnosperm ovule is made of the nucellus protected by envelopes. One cell of the nucellus differentiates into the megaspore mother cell.\nThis cell divides meiotically to form four megaspores.\nThe integument only protects the nucellus. The pollen tube and microsporangium belong to the male side.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cyanobacteria, also called blue green algae, are no longer treated as algae under Kingdom Plantae.\nReason (R): Cyanobacteria are chlorophyll-bearing autotrophic organisms.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Cyanobacteria (blue green algae) are now placed in Kingdom Monera, not among the algae of Plantae.\nR is true. Cyanobacteria are chlorophyll-bearing, photosynthetic autotrophs.\nHowever, R does not explain A. They were moved because they are prokaryotic Monerans. Photosynthetic autotrophy is actually a trait they share with true algae, so it cannot be the reason for separating them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about bryophytes are correct?\n(a) Mosses, along with lichens, are among the first organisms to colonise bare rocks.\n(b) Mosses decompose rocks, making the substrate suitable for higher plants.\n(c) Dense mats of mosses reduce the impact of falling rain and prevent soil erosion.\n(d) Bryophytes in general are of great economic importance.\n(e) Some mosses provide food for herbaceous mammals and birds.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: mosses and lichens are among the first colonisers of bare rock.\n(b) Correct: mosses decompose rock and make the substrate suitable for higher plants.\n(c) Correct: dense moss mats reduce the impact of falling rain and prevent soil erosion.\n(d) Incorrect: bryophytes in general are of little economic importance, though they matter ecologically.\n(e) Correct: some mosses are food for herbaceous mammals and birds.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the kingdoms in Whittaker's (1969) five kingdom classification?","image":"","options":["Monera","Protista","Archaea","Plantae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Whittaker's (1969) five kingdoms are Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia.\nArchaea (archaebacteria) are placed inside kingdom Monera in this system. Archaea as a separate group comes from later schemes such as Woese's three-domain system.\nMonera, Protista and Plantae are all genuine kingdoms in Whittaker's scheme.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Besides their usual aquatic habitats, some algae live in association with animals. Which animal is cited in NCERT as an example?","image":"","options":["Sloth bear","Honey bee","Earthworm","Sea anemone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that algae are mostly aquatic but can also occur in other habitats. These include moist stones, soil and wood.\nSome algae live in association with fungi, forming lichens.\nOthers live in association with animals, and the example NCERT gives is algae growing on the sloth bear.\nThe other animals listed (honey bee, earthworm, sea anemone) are not the example given in NCERT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cyanobacteria, also called blue-green algae, are no longer placed among the algae in Kingdom Plantae.\nStatement II: The classification of flowering plants given by George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker is an artificial system based mainly on androecium structure.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Cyanobacteria are prokaryotes placed in Monera, so they are no longer treated as algae within Plantae.\nStatement II is false. Bentham and Hooker gave a natural system based on many characters. The artificial system based mainly on the androecium was given by Linnaeus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In Cycas, small specialised roots called coralloid roots are associated with which of the following?","image":"","options":["Mycorrhizal fungi that help in phosphate absorption","Nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria","Nitrogen-fixing Rhizobium bacteria in root nodules","Red algae living as epiphytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the coralloid roots of Cycas are associated with $N_2$-fixing cyanobacteria.\nOption 1 is the tempting trap. Mycorrhizal fungal association is the feature mentioned for Pinus roots, not for coralloid roots.\nRhizobium root nodules are typical of legumes, and red algae play no role here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following brown algae has a simple, branched, filamentous body rather than a profusely branched massive one?","image":"","options":["Laminaria","Ectocarpus","Sargassum","Fucus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that brown algae vary greatly in form and size. They range from simple branched, filamentous forms such as Ectocarpus to profusely branched forms such as kelps, which may reach about 100 m.\nLaminaria (a kelp), Sargassum and Fucus have large, complex thalli. These thalli are differentiated into holdfast, stipe and frond, so none of them is filamentous.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Small leaves called microphylls are characteristic of which of the following pteridophytes?","image":"","options":["Dryopteris","Adiantum","Selaginella","Pteris"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: pteridophyte leaves are either small (microphylls), as in Selaginella, or large (macrophylls), as in ferns.\nSelaginella is a lycopsid with small leaves, so it bears microphylls.\nDryopteris, Adiantum and Pteris are all ferns, so they bear macrophylls.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct for Rhodophyceae?\n(i) Most members are marine, with greater concentrations in warmer areas.\n(ii) They are restricted to well-lighted regions close to the water surface.\n(iii) Asexual reproduction occurs by non-motile spores.\n(iv) Sexual reproduction is isogamous.\n(v) Sexual reproduction is followed by complex post-fertilisation developments.\n(vi) They possess chlorophyll a and d.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: most red algae are marine, with greater concentrations in warmer areas.\n(ii) Incorrect: they occur near the surface and also at great depths where little light penetrates.\n(iii) Correct: asexual reproduction is by non-motile spores.\n(iv) Incorrect: sexual reproduction is oogamous, not isogamous.\n(v) Correct: fertilisation is followed by complex post-fertilisation developments.\n(vi) Correct: red algae have chlorophyll a and d, plus phycoerythrin.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'gymnosperm' means 'naked seed'. This name reflects which feature of these plants?","image":"","options":["The ovules lack integuments, so the nucellus is directly exposed","The ovules are not enclosed by an ovary wall, either before or after fertilisation","The seeds are enclosed in a fruit that splits open at maturity","The seeds lack an embryo and food reserves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"'Gymnosperm' means 'naked seed'. The ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall, either before or after fertilisation, so the seeds formed are exposed.\nOption 1 is the tempting wrong choice. Gymnosperm ovules do have integuments around the nucellus. Only the ovary wall is missing.\nThere are no fruits in gymnosperms, and their seeds do contain an embryo and food reserves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The thallus of Marchantia is dorsiventral and lies closely appressed to the substrate.\nStatement II: The leafy gametophyte of a moss is attached to the soil by unicellular, unbranched rhizoids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The Marchantia (liverwort) thallus is dorsiventral and lies closely appressed to the substrate.\nStatement II is incorrect. The leafy gametophyte of a moss is attached to the soil by multicellular, branched rhizoids, not unicellular, unbranched ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In members of Rhodophyceae, food is stored as:","image":"","options":["Laminarin","Mannitol","Floridean starch","Starch in pyrenoids along with protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: red algae (Rhodophyceae) store food as floridean starch, which is structurally very similar to amylopectin and glycogen.\nWhy the others fail: laminarin and mannitol are the reserve foods of brown algae. Starch held in pyrenoids along with protein is typical of green algae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Algin\n(b) Carrageen\n(c) Floridean starch\n(d) Laminarin\nList-II\n(i) Stored food of brown algae\n(ii) Hydrocolloid obtained commercially from red algae\n(iii) Gelatinous coating on the outside of brown algal cell walls\n(iv) Reserve food of red algae, structurally similar to amylopectin and glycogen","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Algin → (iii): it is the gelatinous coating on the outside of brown algal cell walls.\n(b) Carrageen → (ii): it is a hydrocolloid obtained commercially from red algae.\n(c) Floridean starch → (iv): it is the reserve food of red algae, structurally similar to amylopectin and glycogen.\n(d) Laminarin → (i): it is a stored food of brown algae.\nCommon trap: algin is not a stored food, so do not pair it with (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"An alga stores food as mannitol and produces pyriform gametes with two laterally attached flagella. Which other feature would you expect to find in this alga?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll a and b, with pyrenoids containing starch and protein","A cell wall containing cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters","Flagella 2–8 in number, equal and apically inserted","Chlorophyll a and c along with fucoxanthin, and a cellulosic wall coated with algin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Two clues identify the alga. Mannitol storage and pyriform gametes with two lateral flagella are hallmarks of Phaeophyceae (brown algae).\nBrown algae contain chlorophyll a and c plus the pigment fucoxanthin. Their cellulosic cell wall is usually covered by a gelatinous coating of algin. Option (4) matches this.\nOptions (1) and (3) describe Chlorophyceae (green algae), which have chlorophyll a, b and 2–8 equal apical flagella.\nOption (2) is the red-algal cell wall.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The classification of flowering plants given by George Bentham and Joseph Dalton Hooker was an artificial system based mainly on the structure of the androecium.\nStatement II: Natural classification systems consider internal features such as anatomy, embryology and phytochemistry in addition to external features.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The artificial system based mainly on the androecium was given by Linnaeus.\nBentham and Hooker proposed a natural classification of flowering plants, not an artificial one.\nStatement II is correct. Natural systems use external features plus internal features such as ultrastructure, anatomy, embryology and phytochemistry.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for gymnosperms.\nList-I\n(A) Pollen grain\n(B) Ovule\n(C) Female gametophyte\n(D) Megasporophyll\nList-II\n(I) Bears two or more archegonia\n(II) Nucellus together with its protective envelopes\n(III) Reduced male gametophyte\n(IV) Bears ovules and may cluster to form female cones\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – III: The pollen grain is the highly reduced male gametophyte.\nB – II: The ovule is the nucellus together with its protective envelopes.\nC – I: The multicellular female gametophyte bears two or more archegonia.\nD – IV: Megasporophylls bear ovules and may cluster to form female cones.\nOnly option (1) gives all four pairs correctly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Algin\n(b) Agar\n(c) Chlorella\n(d) Peat\nList-II\n(i) Sphagnum\n(ii) Brown algae\n(iii) Gelidium\n(iv) Protein-rich food supplement used by space travellers\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Algin is a hydrocolloid obtained from brown algae, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Agar is obtained from the red algae Gelidium and Gracilaria, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) Chlorella is a protein-rich unicellular alga used as a food supplement, even by space travellers, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) Peat is obtained from the moss Sphagnum, so (d)-(i).\nOption (2) wrongly swaps algin and agar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following algae show oogamous sexual reproduction?\nVolvox, Spirogyra, Fucus, Eudorina, Ulothrix, Polysiphonia","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oogamy is fertilisation between a large non-motile egg and a small motile male gamete.\nOogamous: Volvox, Fucus and Polysiphonia. Red algae such as Polysiphonia are always oogamous.\nNot oogamous: Spirogyra and Ulothrix are isogamous, and Eudorina is anisogamous (its gametes differ in size).\nThat gives 3 oogamous algae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Gemmae\nB. Primary protonema\nC. Leafy stage of moss\nD. Capsule\nList-II\nI. Site where spores are formed after meiosis\nII. Develops directly from a spore\nIII. Asexual buds in receptacles on the liverwort thallus\nIV. Bears the sex organs in mosses\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–III: Gemmae are asexual buds formed in small receptacles (gemma cups) on the liverwort thallus.\nB–II: The primary protonema develops directly from a spore.\nC–IV: The leafy stage of a moss bears the antheridia and archegonia.\nD–I: Spore mother cells in the capsule undergo meiosis to form spores.\nThe matching set is A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (green alga) with List-II (nature of gametes) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\n(a) Ulothrix\n(b) Spirogyra\n(c) Eudorina\n(d) Volvox\nList-II\n(i) Non-flagellated gametes of similar size\n(ii) Large non-motile female gamete and smaller motile male gamete\n(iii) Flagellated gametes of similar size\n(iv) Gametes dissimilar in size","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Ulothrix is isogamous with flagellated gametes of similar size, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Spirogyra is isogamous with non-flagellated gametes of similar size, so it matches (i).\n(c) Eudorina is anisogamous, with gametes dissimilar in size, so it matches (iv).\n(d) Volvox is oogamous, with a large non-motile female gamete and a small motile male gamete, so it matches (ii).\nThe common error is swapping Ulothrix and Spirogyra, since both are isogamous. Flagella decide between them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Selaginella and Salvinia are heterosporous, producing large megaspores and small microspores.\nStatement II: In heterosporous pteridophytes, the megaspores germinate to form male gametophytes and the microspores form female gametophytes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Selaginella and Salvinia are heterosporous. They produce large megaspores and small microspores.\nStatement II is incorrect because it reverses the roles. Megaspores germinate into female gametophytes, and microspores germinate into male gametophytes.\nSo only Statement I is correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pteridophytes are heterosporous?\nSelaginella, Salvinia, Pteris, Lycopodium, Adiantum, Equisetum","image":"","options":["1","3","4","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Heterosporous pteridophytes produce two kinds of spores, megaspores and microspores.\nHeterosporous here: Selaginella and Salvinia.\nHomosporous here: Pteris, Lycopodium, Adiantum and Equisetum, like most pteridophytes.\nWatch out for Lycopodium. It belongs to Lycopsida along with Selaginella, but it is homosporous.\nCount = 2.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical brown alga, the leaf-like photosynthetic organ of the plant body is called the:","image":"","options":["Holdfast","Stipe","Frond","Thallus cup"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The plant body of a typical brown alga has three parts:\n- the holdfast, which attaches it to the substratum;\n- the stipe, which is the stalk;\n- the frond, which is the leaf-like photosynthetic organ.\nThe stipe can look like a tempting answer, but it is only the supporting stalk. 'Thallus cup' is not a part of the brown-algal body.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Porphyra, Laminaria and Sargassum, all of which are used as food, belong to the brown algae.\nStatement II: Agar is used in the preparation of ice-creams and jellies.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Porphyra, Laminaria and Sargassum are all edible marine algae. Laminaria and Sargassum are brown algae (Phaeophyceae), but Porphyra is a red alga (Rhodophyceae).\nStatement II is correct: agar is used in ice-creams and jellies.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In the life cycle of a moss, the leafy stage develops:","image":"","options":["Directly from a germinating spore","From the foot of the sporophyte","As a lateral bud from the secondary protonema","From a gemma produced in a gemma cup"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The moss gametophyte has two stages.\nThe first stage is the protonema, which develops directly from the spore. This is why option (1) is a trap.\nThe second stage is the leafy stage. It develops as a lateral bud from the secondary protonema.\nGemmae, option (4), are asexual buds of liverworts and do not give rise to the leafy stage in the moss life cycle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Pinus, the male and female cones are borne on the same tree.\nStatement II: In Cycas, the male cones and the megasporophylls are also borne on the same tree.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: Pinus is monoecious. Its male and female cones are borne on the same tree.\nStatement II is incorrect: Cycas is dioecious. Its male cones and megasporophylls are borne on different trees.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (commercial product or use) with List-II (source) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\n(A) Algin\n(B) Carrageen\n(C) Agar\n(D) Protein-rich food supplement used by space travellers\nList-II\n(I) Chlorella\n(II) Gelidium\n(III) Brown algae\n(IV) Red algae","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Algin is a hydrocolloid from brown algae → III.\n(B) Carrageen comes from red algae → IV.\n(C) Agar is obtained from Gelidium (and Gracilaria) → II.\n(D) Chlorella, a unicellular alga, is the protein-rich supplement used by space travellers → I.\nOption (1) is wrong because it swaps algin and carrageen.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the economic importance of algae is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Algae increase the level of dissolved oxygen in their immediate environment.","Algin is a hydrocolloid obtained from red algae such as Gelidium.","Many species of Porphyra are used as food.","Agar is used in the preparation of ice-creams and jellies."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: algae photosynthesise, so they raise the dissolved oxygen around them.\n(2) Incorrect: algin is a hydrocolloid from brown algae. Gelidium is a red alga and is a source of agar, not algin.\n(3) Correct: many species of Porphyra (a red alga) are eaten as food.\n(4) Correct: agar is used to make ice-creams and jellies.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants are heterosporous?\nSelaginella, Salvinia, Equisetum, Dryopteris, Pinus, Cycas, Adiantum","image":"","options":["4","2","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Heterosporous plants produce two kinds of spores: small microspores and large megaspores.\nPteridophytes: Selaginella and Salvinia are heterosporous. Equisetum, Dryopteris and Adiantum are homosporous.\nGymnosperms: all are heterosporous, so Pinus and Cycas both count.\nTotal = 2 + 2 = 4.\nDon't count only the pteridophytes (giving 2). Gymnosperms must be included.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The prothallus of a fern is best described as:","image":"","options":["A diploid, dependent sporophyte attached to the gametophyte","A haploid, free-living, mostly photosynthetic thalloid gametophyte","A compact cone of sporophylls bearing sporangia","A diploid structure formed directly from the zygote"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Spores germinate to form the prothallus.\nThe prothallus is the gametophyte: small, multicellular, free-living, mostly photosynthetic and thalloid. Because it develops from a spore, it is haploid.\nOptions (1) and (4) describe diploid structures, which belong to the sporophyte stage.\nOption (3) describes a strobilus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Numerical taxonomy\n(b) Cytotaxonomy\n(c) Chemotaxonomy\n(d) Artificial system\nList-II\n(i) Chromosome number, structure and behaviour\n(ii) A few superficial morphological characters\n(iii) All observable characters coded and given equal importance\n(iv) Chemical constituents of the plant","image":"","options":["a-(iii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(ii)","a-(i), b-(iii), c-(iv), d-(ii)","a-(iii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(ii)","a-(ii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Numerical taxonomy: all observable characters are coded and given equal importance, then processed by computer → (iii).\n(b) Cytotaxonomy: uses chromosome number, structure and behaviour → (i).\n(c) Chemotaxonomy: uses the chemical constituents of the plant → (iv).\n(d) Artificial system: based on a few superficial morphological characters → (ii).\nThe correct matching is a-(iii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(ii), which is option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A student argues: 'Since the main plant body of a bryophyte produces gametes, it must produce them by meiosis, as animals do.' Which of the following best corrects the student?","image":"","options":["The gametophyte is diploid, and meiosis in it produces haploid antherozoids and eggs.","The zygote undergoes meiosis directly to form a new haploid gametophyte, so gametes need no reduction division.","The gametophyte is already haploid, so its gametes are formed without meiosis; meiosis occurs only in some cells of the sporophyte to form spores.","Meiosis occurs in the archegonium to form the egg, but antherozoids are formed without meiosis."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The bryophyte gametophyte is already haploid, so it forms gametes by mitosis, not meiosis.\nMeiosis occurs only in some cells of the diploid sporophyte, where it produces haploid spores.\nOption (1) is wrong because the gametophyte is not diploid.\nOption (2) is wrong because the zygote does not undergo meiosis directly.\nOption (4) is wrong because the egg is not formed by meiosis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A Prothallus is","image":"","options":["A structure in pteridophytes formed before the thallus develops","A sporophytic free living structure formed in pteridophytes","A gametophyte free living structure formed in pteridophytes","A primitive structure formed after fertilization in pteridophytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In pteridophytes, a spore germinates into a small, multicellular, free-living, mostly photosynthetic thalloid gametophyte. This is the prothallus.\nThe prothallus bears the antheridia and archegonia.\nIt is not a sporophyte, and it forms before fertilisation, not after it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are considered in a natural system of classification, as described in NCERT?\n(i) External features\n(ii) Ultrastructure\n(iii) Anatomy\n(iv) Embryology\n(v) Phytochemistry\n(vi) Assigning numbers and codes to characters and processing the data by computer","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says natural systems use natural affinities. These come from external features and from internal features.\n(i) External features: considered.\n(ii) Ultrastructure, (iii) Anatomy, (iv) Embryology, (v) Phytochemistry: all are internal features the natural system considers.\n(vi) Coding characters and processing them by computer is numerical taxonomy, not the natural system.\nSo five items are considered.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following pairs of a commercially used hydrocolloid and its algal source is correctly matched?","image":"","options":["Algin – red algae","Carrageen – red algae","Agar – Laminaria","Algin – green algae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Carrageen is obtained from red algae, so option (2) is correctly matched.\nWhy the other options fail:\n- Algin comes from brown algae, not red algae (option 1) or green algae (option 4).\n- Agar comes from the red algae Gelidium and Gracilaria, not from the brown alga Laminaria (option 3).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In the life cycle of a bryophyte, which of the following pairs consists ONLY of diploid cells?","image":"","options":["Antherozoid and zygote","Rhizoid cell and spore","Zygote and a sporophyte cell that undergoes meiosis","Egg and a cell of the sporophyte"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The zygote is diploid (2n), and every sporophyte cell formed from it is also diploid, including the cells that undergo meiosis.\nAntherozoids, eggs, rhizoid cells and spores all belong to the haploid (n) phase.\nTherefore options (1), (2) and (4) each contain a haploid cell. Only option (3) has two diploid cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (classification approach) with List-II (its basis).\nList-I\nA. System of Linnaeus\nB. System of Bentham and Hooker\nC. Phylogenetic system\nD. Numerical taxonomy\nList-II\n(i) Assumes that organisms of the same taxon share a common ancestor\n(ii) Assigns codes to all observable characters and gives each character equal importance\n(iii) Relies on gross morphology and the structure of the androecium\n(iv) Uses natural affinities, including internal features such as embryology and phytochemistry\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-iii: Linnaeus gave an artificial system based on gross morphology and the structure of the androecium.\nB-iv: Bentham and Hooker gave a natural system based on natural affinities, including internal features.\nC-i: Phylogenetic systems assume that members of the same taxon share a common ancestor.\nD-ii: Numerical taxonomy codes all observable characters and gives each equal weight.\nDo not swap C and D. Equal weighting of coded characters belongs to numerical taxonomy, not to phylogenetic systems.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes a freshwater, unbranched filamentous alga under the microscope. Its cells have spiral, ribbon-shaped chloroplasts with pyrenoids, and a cellulose–pectose wall. During sexual reproduction, two non-flagellated gametes of similar size fuse. The alga is most likely:","image":"","options":["Ulothrix","Ectocarpus","Polysiphonia","Spirogyra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The clues point to a green alga: spiral ribbon-shaped chloroplasts with pyrenoids, a cellulose–pectose wall, an unbranched freshwater filament, and isogamy with non-flagellated gametes. This identifies Spirogyra.\nUlothrix fails because its isogametes are flagellated.\nEctocarpus fails because it is a brown alga with pear-shaped biflagellate gametes.\nPolysiphonia fails because it is an oogamous red alga with floridean starch.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Phaeophyceae is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Kelps are profusely branched forms that may reach a height of about 100 metres","In oogamous species, the union of gametes may take place within the oogonium","Brown algae are exclusively marine, with no freshwater representatives","The protoplast contains a centrally located vacuole and nucleus in addition to plastids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Kelps are profusely branched and may reach about 100 m in height.\n(2) Correct. In oogamous species, fusion of gametes may occur in water or within the oogonium.\n(3) Incorrect. Brown algae are found mainly in marine habitats, but Table 3.1 also lists rare freshwater and brackish-water forms, so 'exclusively marine' is wrong.\n(4) Correct. Besides plastids, the protoplast has a central vacuole and nucleus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature is shared by Selaginella and Pinus but is absent in Dryopteris?","image":"","options":["Dominant sporophytic plant body with vascular tissues","Presence of archegonia as female sex organs","Production of naked seeds","Production of two kinds of spores (heterospory)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Selaginella is a heterosporous pteridophyte and Pinus is a gymnosperm. Both produce microspores and megaspores, which is heterospory.\nDryopteris is a fern and is homosporous, so it lacks this feature.\nWhy the other options fail:\n- A dominant vascular sporophyte (option 1) and archegonia (option 2) are found in all three plants.\n- Naked seeds (option 3) occur only in Pinus.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Fungal association with roots in the form of mycorrhiza is a characteristic example seen in which gymnosperm?","image":"","options":["Cycas","Pinus","Sequoia only in its seeds","Selaginella"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT gives Pinus as the gymnosperm whose roots associate with fungi to form mycorrhiza.\nWhy the other options fail:\n- Cycas is the tempting trap. Its coralloid roots are associated with nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria, not fungi.\n- Mycorrhiza is a root association, so it cannot be found 'only in seeds' (Sequoia).\n- Selaginella is a pteridophyte, not a gymnosperm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Species of Sphagnum provide peat that is used as packing material for trans-shipment of living material. This use is mainly due to Sphagnum's:","image":"","options":["ability to fix atmospheric nitrogen","high capacity to hold water","production of antibiotic substances","rigid lignified tissues that resist crushing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sphagnum, a moss, forms peat that can absorb and retain large amounts of water.\nThis keeps living material moist during trans-shipment, so peat makes a good packing material.\nOption (4) is wrong because bryophytes lack lignified vascular tissue.\nOptions (1) and (3) are not the NCERT basis for this use.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"The rigid cell wall of a typical green alga is made of:","image":"","options":["An inner layer of pectose and an outer layer of cellulose","An inner layer of cellulose and an outer layer of pectose","Cellulose covered by a gelatinous coating of algin","Cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: green algae have a rigid cell wall with an inner layer of cellulose and an outer layer of pectose.\nOption (1) is a trap because it reverses the two layers.\nOption (3) fails because a gelatinous coating of algin over cellulose is a brown algal (Phaeophyceae) feature.\nOption (4) fails because cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters make up the red algal (Rhodophyceae) wall.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about gymnosperms is correct?","image":"","options":["Pinus bears coralloid roots associated with nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria","In Cycas, male cones and megasporophylls are borne on the same tree","Cycas has small specialised coralloid roots associated with $N_2$-fixing cyanobacteria","The ovules of gymnosperms are enclosed within an ovary wall before fertilisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: Pinus roots form mycorrhizae with fungi. They do not have coralloid roots.\n(2) Incorrect: Cycas is dioecious, so male cones and megasporophylls are borne on different trees.\n(3) Correct: Cycas has small, specialised coralloid roots associated with $N_2$-fixing cyanobacteria.\n(4) Incorrect: gymnosperm ovules are naked. They are not enclosed in an ovary wall before or after fertilisation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In Marchantia, gemmae are best described as:","image":"","options":["Haploid unicellular spores formed by meiosis inside the capsule","Green, multicellular asexual buds that develop in gemma cups on the thallus","Diploid multicellular buds that grow on the seta of the sporophyte","Flagellated male gametes released from the antheridia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes gemmae as green, multicellular, asexual buds. They develop in small receptacles called gemma cups on the gametophytic thallus.\nThey detach from the parent body and germinate into new individuals.\n(1) is tempting but wrong: gemmae are multicellular, and they are not formed by meiosis.\n(3) is wrong because gemmae are haploid and come from the gametophyte, not the sporophyte.\n(4) is wrong because flagellated male gametes are antherozoids, not gemmae.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Algae lower the dissolved oxygen in their immediate surroundings.\n(ii) Sargassum is among the marine algae used as food.\n(iii) Algin is a water-holding substance produced by brown algae.\n(iv) Chlorella is a multicellular, filamentous alga used as a food supplement.\n(v) Gracilaria is a source of agar.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Wrong: photosynthesis increases dissolved oxygen, it does not lower it.\n(ii) Correct: Sargassum is an edible marine alga.\n(iii) Correct: algin is a water-holding substance from brown algae.\n(iv) Wrong: Chlorella is unicellular, not multicellular or filamentous.\n(v) Correct: Gracilaria is a source of agar.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes oogamy from anisogamy as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["In oogamy the two gametes are similar in size, whereas in anisogamy they differ in size","In oogamy the female gamete is large and non-motile and the male gamete is small and motile, whereas anisogamy only involves gametes dissimilar in size","In oogamy both gametes are non-motile, whereas in anisogamy both gametes are always non-motile but unequal","Oogamy occurs only in colonial algae, whereas anisogamy occurs only in filamentous algae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Anisogamy (e.g., Eudorina) is the fusion of two gametes dissimilar in size.\nOogamy (e.g., Volvox, Fucus) is the fusion of a large, non-motile (static) female gamete with a smaller, motile male gamete.\nOption (2) states this correctly.\n(1) wrongly makes oogamous gametes similar in size.\n(3) wrongly calls both gametes non-motile.\n(4) fails because Fucus is oogamous but not colonial.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about algae is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Some algae grow on moist stones, soils and wood","Some algae live in association with fungi, forming lichens","Cyanobacteria are classified as algae because they are called blue green algae","A few marine algae, such as kelps, form massive plant bodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: some algae grow on moist stones, soil and wood.\n(2) Correct: algae associate with fungi to form lichens.\n(3) Incorrect: cyanobacteria are prokaryotes placed in Monera. Despite the name 'blue green algae', they are excluded from Plantae and are not true algae.\n(4) Correct: kelps form massive plant bodies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (alga) with List-II (type of sexual reproduction):\nList-I\nA. Ulothrix\nB. Spirogyra\nC. Eudorina\nD. Fucus\nList-II\nI. Fusion of gametes dissimilar in size\nII. Fusion of a large non-motile female gamete with a small motile male gamete\nIII. Fusion of flagellated gametes similar in size\nIV. Fusion of non-flagellated gametes similar in size\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Ulothrix: isogamy with flagellated gametes of similar size, so A-III.\nB. Spirogyra: isogamy with non-flagellated gametes of similar size, so B-IV.\nC. Eudorina: anisogamy, in which the gametes differ in size, so C-I.\nD. Fucus: oogamy, with a large non-motile egg and a small motile male gamete, so D-II.\nThe common trap is swapping Ulothrix and Spirogyra (option 2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In gymnosperms, the pollen grain represents which of the following?","image":"","options":["A diploid microsporophyll","A free-living male gametophyte","A highly reduced male gametophyte confined to a few cells","A megaspore that has not yet germinated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: In gymnosperms, each microspore develops into a male gametophyte that is highly reduced and confined to only a few cells. This reduced gametophyte is called the pollen grain.\nOption (2) is wrong because gymnosperm gametophytes are not free-living. They depend on the sporophyte.\nThe pollen grain is haploid, so it is not a diploid microsporophyll, and it is not a megaspore.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Red algae lack flagella.\n(b) The cell wall of red algae contains cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters.\n(c) Brown algae possess chlorophyll a and c along with fucoxanthin.\n(d) Green algae bear 2-8 equal, apically inserted flagella.\n(e) Zoospores of brown algae are pear-shaped with two unequal, laterally attached flagella.\n(f) Floridean starch is very similar to amylopectin and glycogen in structure.\n(g) Sexual reproduction in red algae is isogamous with simple post-fertilisation development.","image":"","options":["5","6","7","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: Rhodophyceae have no flagella.\n(b) Correct: the red algal wall has cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters.\n(c) Correct: brown algae have chlorophyll a, c and fucoxanthin.\n(d) Correct: green algae bear 2-8 equal, apical flagella.\n(e) Correct: brown algal zoospores are pear-shaped, with two unequal lateral flagella.\n(f) Correct: floridean starch resembles amylopectin and glycogen.\n(g) Incorrect: red algae are oogamous and show complex post-fertilisation development.\nSix statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are characteristic of Chlorophyceae?\n• Dominance of chlorophyll a and b\n• Food stored as floridean starch\n• Pyrenoids located in chloroplasts\n• Cell wall covered by a gelatinous coating of algin\n• 2–8 equal, apically inserted flagella\n• Fucoxanthin as the major accessory pigment\n• Asexual reproduction by flagellated zoospores produced in zoosporangia","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll a and b dominant: Correct for green algae.\nFloridean starch: Incorrect, as this is the red algal reserve.\nPyrenoids in chloroplasts: Correct.\nAlgin coating: Incorrect, as this is a brown algal feature.\n2–8 equal, apical flagella: Correct.\nFucoxanthin: Incorrect, as this is the brown algal pigment.\nFlagellated zoospores formed in zoosporangia: Correct.\nThat gives 4 green algal features.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In green algae, the photosynthetic pigments are localised in definite chloroplasts.\nReason (R): Green algae, like the blue-green algae, lack membrane-bound organelles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Green algae are eukaryotes, and their pigments lie in definite chloroplasts. These may be discoid, plate-like, reticulate, cup-shaped, spiral or ribbon-shaped.\nR is false. Being eukaryotes, green algae do have membrane-bound organelles. Only cyanobacteria (blue-green algae), which are prokaryotes, lack them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In gymnosperms, the pollen grain represents which of the following?","image":"","options":["A microspore mother cell","A highly reduced male gametophyte confined to a few cells","A male sporophyte","A male gamete that directly fuses with the egg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each haploid microspore develops into a male gametophyte reduced to only a few cells, and this structure is the pollen grain.\nIt is not a gamete, so option 4 is wrong. The male gametes form within the gametophyte and are carried by the pollen tube.\nIt is not a microspore mother cell (diploid) and not a sporophyte.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about reproduction in gymnosperms are correct?\n(i) The megaspore mother cell divides meiotically to form four megaspores.\n(ii) All four megaspores develop into female gametophytes within the ovule.\n(iii) The female gametophyte is multicellular and bears two or more archegonia.\n(iv) The female gametophyte is retained within the megasporangium.\n(v) Pollen grains are carried to the ovules mainly by water.\n(vi) After fertilisation, the ovules develop into seeds enclosed within fruits.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the megaspore mother cell divides by meiosis to give four megaspores.\n(ii) Incorrect: only one megaspore develops into the female gametophyte.\n(iii) Correct: the female gametophyte is multicellular and bears two or more archegonia.\n(iv) Correct: it is retained within the megasporangium.\n(v) Incorrect: pollen is carried by air currents, not water.\n(vi) Incorrect: gymnosperm seeds are naked and not enclosed in fruits.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The sporophyte of a bryophyte is not free-living.\nReason (R): The bryophyte sporophyte remains attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and derives nourishment from it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The bryophyte sporophyte cannot live independently.\nR is true. The sporophyte stays attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and draws nourishment from it.\nR explains A. The sporophyte is not free-living precisely because it depends on the gametophyte for food.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Bryophytes are called the 'amphibians of the plant kingdom' because they:","image":"","options":["live in soil but depend on water for sexual reproduction","live in water but carry out photosynthesis only on land","have a gametophyte that lives in water and a sporophyte that lives on land","lack vascular tissue and so absorb water through their whole surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: bryophytes are the 'amphibians of the plant kingdom' because they live in soil but depend on water for sexual reproduction.\nTheir antherozoids must swim through water to reach the archegonium.\nOption (4) states a real feature (no vascular tissue), but that is not the reason for the name.\nOptions (2) and (3) invent habitat splits that do not exist.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Both Ulothrix and Spirogyra show isogamous sexual reproduction.\nStatement II: The gametes of Spirogyra bear flagella and are motile.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Both Ulothrix and Spirogyra fuse gametes of similar size, so both are isogamous.\nStatement II is incorrect. Spirogyra gametes are non-flagellated and non-motile. Flagellated isogametes are seen in Ulothrix instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Algae are of paramount importance as primary producers in aquatic habitats.\nReason (R): Through photosynthesis, algae produce energy-rich compounds that form the basis of the food cycles of all aquatic animals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: algae are the main primary producers in aquatic habitats.\nR is true: by photosynthesis, algae make energy-rich compounds that form the base of aquatic food cycles.\nR explains A, because being a primary producer means making the food that the rest of the food chain depends on.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In the life cycle of a moss such as Funaria, the leafy stage develops:","image":"","options":["Directly from a germinating spore","From the secondary protonema as a lateral bud","From the capsule of the sporophyte after meiosis","From a gemma detached from a gemma cup"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: in mosses the spore germinates first into the protonema, a creeping, green, branched, filamentous juvenile stage.\nThe leafy stage then grows from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud. It bears spirally arranged leaves.\nOption (1) is the common trap. The spore produces the protonema, not the leafy shoot.\nOption (3) fails because the capsule produces spores, not gametophytes. Option (4) fails because gemma cups belong to liverworts such as Marchantia.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Algae are found in many habitats besides water. In which of the following associations do algae occur together with fungi?","image":"","options":["Mycorrhiza","Lichen","Coralloid roots","Root nodules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A lichen is a close association between an alga, the photosynthetic partner, and a fungus, which gives shelter and absorbs water and minerals.\nMycorrhiza is a fungus living with the roots of higher plants such as Pinus. No alga is involved.\nCoralloid roots of Cycas hold cyanobacteria, which are no longer classed as algae. Root nodules of legumes hold bacteria such as Rhizobium.\nSo only the lichen pairs an alga with a fungus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are members of Phaeophyceae?\nEctocarpus, Porphyra, Dictyota, Gracilaria, Sargassum, Polysiphonia, Fucus, Laminaria","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sort each genus by class.\nPhaeophyceae (brown algae): Ectocarpus, Dictyota, Sargassum, Fucus, Laminaria. That makes 5.\nRhodophyceae (red algae): Porphyra, Gracilaria, Polysiphonia. These are often mixed up with brown seaweeds because both are marine, but they are red algae.\nSo 5 members belong to Phaeophyceae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Bryophytes are called 'amphibians of the plant kingdom' because they:","image":"","options":["live in soil but need water for sexual reproduction","have both a haploid and a diploid free-living phase","grow partly submerged in water and partly in air","can photosynthesise both on land and under the sea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Bryophytes live on land, in soil, but their sexual reproduction depends on water.\nTheir biflagellate antherozoids must swim through a film of water to reach the archegonium. This dual dependence on land and water earns them the name 'amphibians'.\nOption (2) fails because the bryophyte sporophyte is not free-living. It stays attached to the gametophyte.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe habitats, not the actual reason for the name.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Algin\nB. Carrageen\nC. Agar\nD. Chlorella\nList-II\n(i) Protein-rich unicellular alga used as a food supplement by space travellers\n(ii) Hydrocolloid obtained from red algae\n(iii) Hydrocolloid obtained from brown algae\n(iv) Product of Gelidium and Gracilaria used to grow microbes\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Algin is the hydrocolloid from brown algae, so A-(iii).\nB. Carrageen is the hydrocolloid from red algae, so B-(ii).\nC. Agar comes from Gelidium and Gracilaria and is used to grow microbes, so C-(iv).\nD. Chlorella is a protein-rich unicellular alga used as a food supplement by space travellers, so D-(i).\nThe common trap is swapping algin and carrageen, which gives option (1).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In mosses, the leafy stage develops directly from the spore.\nReason (R): The protonema of a moss is a creeping, green, branched and frequently filamentous stage.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. The moss spore does not form the leafy stage directly. It first germinates into the protonema.\nThe leafy stage develops later, as a lateral bud on the secondary protonema.\nR is true. The protonema is a creeping, green, branched and frequently filamentous stage.\nSince A is false and R is true, the answer is (4).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The sporophyte of mosses is more elaborate than that of liverworts.\nReason (R): The moss sporophyte is free-living and independent of the gametophyte for nutrition.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: the moss sporophyte is more elaborate than the liverwort sporophyte. Mosses also have an elaborate spore dispersal mechanism.\nR is false: in all bryophytes the sporophyte is not free-living. It stays attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and draws nourishment from it.\nSo A is true but R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair consists only of heterosporous pteridophytes?","image":"","options":["Selaginella and Salvinia","Equisetum and Dryopteris","Psilotum and Pteris","Adiantum and Equisetum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT names Selaginella and Salvinia as heterosporous genera. Both produce megaspores and microspores.\nEquisetum, Dryopteris, Psilotum, Pteris and Adiantum are homosporous, like most pteridophytes.\nSo only option (1) has both members heterosporous.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"According to the comparison of algal classes, the cell wall of red algae is made of:","image":"","options":["Cellulose and algin","Cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters","An inner cellulose layer and an outer pectose layer only","Chitin and cellulose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT Table 3.1 lists the cell wall of Rhodophyceae (red algae) as cellulose, pectin and polysulphate esters.\nOption (1), cellulose and algin, is the brown-algal wall.\nOption (3), an inner cellulose layer with an outer pectose layer, describes green algae.\nOption (4) is wrong because chitin is a fungal wall component, not an algal one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about liverworts is correct?","image":"","options":["The thallus of Marchantia is erect and radially symmetrical.","Gemmae are produced by meiosis inside the capsule.","Gemmae are green, multicellular asexual buds that develop in gemma cups on the thallus.","The sporophyte of a liverwort consists only of a capsule and lacks a foot and seta."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the Marchantia thallus is dorsiventral and closely appressed to the substrate, not erect or radial.\n(2) Incorrect: meiosis in the capsule produces spores, not gemmae. Gemmae are formed mitotically on the gametophyte.\n(3) Correct: gemmae are green, multicellular asexual buds that develop in gemma cups on the thallus.\n(4) Incorrect: the liverwort sporophyte has a foot, a seta and a capsule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (class) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Psilopsida\nB. Lycopsida\nC. Sphenopsida\nD. Pteropsida\nList-II\n(i) Equisetum\n(ii) Adiantum\n(iii) Psilotum\n(iv) Lycopodium\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Psilopsida pairs with Psilotum (iii).\nB. Lycopsida pairs with Lycopodium (iv).\nC. Sphenopsida pairs with Equisetum (i).\nD. Pteropsida pairs with Adiantum (ii), a fern.\nThe usual trap is swapping Psilotum and Lycopodium, as option (2) does.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Four algae P, Q, R and S show the following features of their fusing gametes:\nP: both gametes flagellated and similar in size\nQ: both gametes non-flagellated and similar in size\nR: both gametes motile but dissimilar in size\nS: a large static female gamete and a small motile male gamete\nWhich option correctly gives the type of sexual reproduction and a matching example for each?","image":"","options":["P – isogamy (Ulothrix); Q – anisogamy (Spirogyra); R – anisogamy (Eudorina); S – oogamy (Fucus)","P – isogamy (Ulothrix); Q – isogamy (Spirogyra); R – anisogamy (Eudorina); S – oogamy (Volvox)","P – isogamy (Spirogyra); Q – isogamy (Ulothrix); R – oogamy (Eudorina); S – anisogamy (Volvox)","P – anisogamy (Ulothrix); Q – isogamy (Spirogyra); R – oogamy (Fucus); S – oogamy (Volvox)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"P: The gametes are flagellated and equal in size, so this is isogamy (Ulothrix).\nQ: The gametes are non-flagellated but still equal in size, so this is also isogamy (Spirogyra). Being non-motile does not make it anisogamy.\nR: The gametes are motile but unequal in size, so this is anisogamy (Eudorina).\nS: A large static female gamete fuses with a small motile male gamete, so this is oogamy (Volvox).\nOnly option 2 gets all four right. Option 1 wrongly calls Q anisogamous.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In mosses, the sporophyte is free-living and independent of the gametophyte for its nutrition.\nStatement II: In mosses, the leafy stage develops from the secondary protonema as a lateral bud.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. In bryophytes, including mosses, the sporophyte is not free-living. It stays attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and depends on it for nutrition.\nStatement II is correct. The leafy gametophore of a moss develops as a lateral bud from the secondary protonema.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"In a gymnosperm such as Pinus, which of the following pairs consists of structures that are BOTH haploid?","image":"","options":["Nucellus and megaspore","Female gametophyte and pollen grain","Megasporophyll and egg","Zygote and microspore"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The female gametophyte develops from a haploid megaspore, so it is haploid ($n$).\nThe pollen grain is the reduced male gametophyte formed from a haploid microspore, so it is also haploid.\nThe nucellus, the megasporophyll and the zygote belong to the sporophyte, so they are diploid ($2n$). This rules out options 1, 3 and 4, even though each of those pairs contains one haploid partner.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Algin\n(b) Floridean starch\n(c) Agar\n(d) Laminarin\nList-II\n(i) Reserve food of red algae, structurally similar to amylopectin and glycogen\n(ii) Commercial product of Gelidium and Gracilaria used to grow microbes\n(iii) Complex carbohydrate stored as food in brown algae\n(iv) Hydrocolloid forming the gelatinous coating on brown algal cell walls\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Algin is the hydrocolloid forming the gelatinous coating outside brown-algal cell walls, so a-iv.\n(b) Floridean starch is the red-algal reserve, similar to amylopectin and glycogen, so b-i.\n(c) Agar is obtained from Gelidium and Gracilaria and is used to grow microbes, so c-ii.\n(d) Laminarin is a complex carbohydrate stored as food in brown algae, so d-iii.\nDo not swap algin and laminarin: both come from brown algae, but one is a wall coating and the other is stored food.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"A plant shows thallus level of organization. It shows rhizoids and is haploid. It needs water to complete its life cycle because the male gametes are motile. Identify the group to which it belongs to","image":"","options":["Pteridophytes","Gymnosperms","Monocots","Bryophytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each clue points to bryophytes:\nthe body is a thallus attached by rhizoids; the dominant plant body is the haploid gametophyte; motile antherozoids need water to reach the egg.\nPteridophytes also need water for fertilisation, but their main plant body is a diploid sporophyte with true roots.\nGymnosperms and monocots are diploid and do not need water for fertilisation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 3 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-KINGDOM-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Kingdom","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Kingdom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Conifers are able to withstand dry and windy conditions with relatively little water loss.\nReason (R): Their needle-like leaves have a reduced surface area, a thick cuticle and sunken stomata.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: conifers tolerate dry, windy conditions and extremes of temperature and humidity.\nR is true: their needle-like leaves have a small surface area, a thick cuticle and sunken stomata.\nEach of these features reduces water loss through transpiration, so R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 3 · Plant Kingdom","difficulty":"medium"}]});
